.\" Manual page for tacctl, the management CLI for TACACS+ (tacquito) and RADIUS (FreeRADIUS).
.\" Render locally with: groff -man -Tascii man/tacctl.1 | less
.TH TACCTL 1 "2026-10-07" "tacctl 0.2.1" "tacctl Manual"
.SH NAME
tacctl \- manage users, groups and scopes for TACACS+ (tacquito) and RADIUS (FreeRADIUS)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tacctl
.I command
.RI [ subcommand ]
.RI [ arguments... ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B tacctl
is a management CLI for network-device AAA. It keeps users,
groups, scopes (named CIDR + shared-secret bundles) and connection filters
in a store of its own,
.IR /etc/tacctl/store.yaml ,
and renders the configuration of every enabled
.I backend
from it: TACACS+ (RFC 8907) served by
.BR tacquito ,
installed by
.BR "tacctl install" ,
and, opt-in, RADIUS served by a tacctl-owned FreeRADIUS instance
.RB ( "tacctl backend enable radius" ).
It installs and upgrades the servers, inspects logs and backups, enrolls
Linux hosts (pam_tacplus or pam_radius_auth), and generates ready-to-paste
device configuration for Cisco IOS/IOS-XE and Juniper Junos, plus a
menu-by-menu setup procedure for WTI console servers, for either protocol.
See
.BR BACKENDS ,
.B "STORE AND GENERATED CONFIGURATION"
and
.BR UPGRADING .
.PP
Most commands require root.
.B tacctl
re-executes itself under
.BR sudo (8)
automatically when invoked by a non-root user.
.BR "tacctl hash" ,
.BR "tacctl version" ,
.B tacctl completion
and
.B tacctl shell
are deliberate exceptions: they run as the invoking user.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS System lifecycle
.TP
.BI "install [--branch " name "] [-y|--yes]"
Install tacctl and the TACACS+ backend. Builds the tacquito binary with the
Go toolchain (installed by
.I /opt/tacctl/bin/tacctl.sh
when it is missing), installs the
.I /usr/local/bin/tacctl
binary (the verified release binary when the clone in
.I /opt/tacctl
is at a release tag, otherwise built from the clone),
installs the shell completion and this manual, creates the
.B tacquito
service user and the state directory
.IR /etc/tacctl ,
seeds the store (built-in groups and users, the scope
.B lab
with RFC 1918 prefixes and a generated shared secret, printed once),
renders
.I /etc/tacquito/tacquito.yaml
from it, installs the systemd units, and enables the service. An existing
store, or an existing
.IR tacquito.yaml ,
is kept instead (the latter goes through the upgrade's store gate). RADIUS
is not installed: see
.BR "backend enable" .
The optional
.BI --branch " name"
flag selects a specific tacctl repository branch for the install. The plan
is shown and confirmed
.RB ( [y/N] );
.B -y
(or
.BR --yes )
answers the confirmation, for scripts.
.IP
Four built-in users are seeded so fresh installs ship with usable
identities (without writing unknown credentials) and with a sink for
Junos internal accounting:
.B engineer
.RB ( superuser ),
.B operator
.RB ( operator ),
.B viewer
.RB ( readonly ),
and
.B root
.RB ( readonly ).
.IP
The first three carry a placeholder hash that never authenticates;
activate one with
.B "tacctl user passwd " <name>
(setting a real password implicitly enables the account).
.B root
is different — a permanent accounting-only sink whose hash stays
disabled for the lifetime of the install. It exists because Junos
internal daemons (mgd, jsd, op-script probes) run non-tty CLI commands
as root and generate accounting packets; without a
.B root
entry tacquito logs an error for every packet. Records of
.B root
with no terminal (port
.BR non\-tty ,
which Junos sends for them,
.B unknown
or empty) are answered with success and not recorded: those are the
device's own sessions, several a minute on Junos. A
.B root
login on the console or over ssh has a port and is recorded, its commands
included.
.B "tacctl user passwd root"
is rejected — root is a Junos built-in intended for local/console use
only and must never authenticate via TACACS+.
.TP
.BI "upgrade [--branch " name "]"
Move tacctl state into
.I /etc/tacctl
(idempotent), pull and rebuild tacquito when upstream or the patch overlay
changed, pull the tacctl repository in
.I /opt/tacctl
(after switching to
.BI --branch " name"
when given) and, when the installed binary was not built from the commit
now checked out, replace it (the verified release binary at a release tag,
otherwise built from the clone) and re-execute it once, install
packages the new release needs, bring every enabled
backend's configuration in line with the release (a re-render from the
store; RADIUS restarts when its files or unit drop-in changed), refresh
system files (a device template you changed is kept; see
.I /etc/tacctl/templates/
under
.BR FILES ),
move an install that has no store yet into the store behind a
gate, and restart tacquito once if its binary, a unit or drop-in, or its
configuration changed (rolling the binary and unit files back if it does not
come up). The shell completion is regenerated by the binary and rewritten
only when its content changed (the output says
.B Updated
or
.BR Unchanged ).
A new README, logrotate file, completion or template restarts
nothing, and neither does an upgrade with nothing new. Listener, log-level and metrics
settings live in
.I tacctl.yaml
and survive. See
.BR UPGRADING .
.TP
.BR "uninstall " [ -y | --yes ]
Remove tacctl and every backend that is enabled or installed: the tacquito
service, units, binaries, configuration and logs; tacctl's FreeRADIUS
configuration, unit drop-in and logs (the FreeRADIUS package stays, with its
own configuration as shipped); the state directory, sudoers rules,
completion, man page and management repository. Offers to preserve backups
and each backend's logs by archiving them to
.IR /root/ .
.B -y
(or
.BR --yes )
skips the confirmation and answers
.B no
to both offers: nothing is preserved.
.TP
.B status
Show service health, authentication statistics, recent errors, and
password-age warnings. With more than one backend enabled each backend has a
labelled section
.RB ( "== Backend: <id> ..." )
holding its service, config, accounting log, drift and activity lines; the
users, backups, security posture and password-age lines that belong to no
backend stay in the general part. With one backend enabled the report is
laid out as a single report. A listener on an IPv6 network
.RB ( tcp6 ", " udp6 )
of any backend triggers the IPv6 ACL parity check.
.TP
.BR "version " [ --long ]
.TQ
.BR --version ", " -v
Print the tacctl version, derived from
.BR git-describe (1)
when the binary was built, so it reflects the exact commit that was built.
.B --long
adds the commit, the build date, the Go version and whether the test knobs
are compiled in
.RB ( "test knobs: off" ).
.TP
.B completion bash|zsh|fish
Print the completion script of that shell on stdout. The script asks
.B "tacctl __complete"
for the words, so completion always matches the installed binary. Live names
(users, groups, scopes, backends, listeners, backups) come from
.BR "sudo -n tacctl" ,
which needs root or the NOPASSWD rule of
.BR "config sudoers install" ;
without it only fixed words are completed. Install and upgrade write the
bash script to
.IR /etc/bash_completion.d/tacctl ;
the zsh and fish scripts are only printed: redirect them to
.I ~/.zfunc/_tacctl
(with that directory on
.B fpath
before
.BR compinit )
or to
.IR ~/.config/fish/completions/tacctl.fish .
.SS User management \fB(tacctl user)\fR
.TP
.B user list
List all users with name, Linux UID (\- when none is assigned), group, status,
password change date and scopes.
.TP
.BI "user show " name
Show detailed user info: group, Linux UID, status, password change date and age,
last login (the most recent of every enabled backend: the TACACS+
accounting log, the RADIUS auth log), Cisco privilege level, Juniper
class, and a hash fingerprint. Read-only -- does not prompt for the
password (unlike
.BR "user verify" ).
.TP
.BI "user add " "name group " [ "--hash " hash ] [ "--scopes " name[,name...] ]
Add a user. Prompts for the password with confirmation. If
.BI --hash " hash"
is given, the bcrypt hash is used directly and no password is prompted
(use this when users self-generate a hash; see
.BR "tacctl hash commands" ).
.BI --scopes " name"[,name...]
grants the user access to those scopes (see
.B SCOPE MANAGEMENT ).
Without
.BR --scopes ,
the user lands in the current default scope. On fresh installs the
default scope is
.B lab
— least-privilege posture, so an operator who forgets
.B --scopes
does not accidentally grant production access.
.PP
Reserved usernames
.RB ( root
and
.BR tacquito )
are rejected: both map to local OS accounts that tacquito resolves outside
the YAML. Create an operator-specific account instead (for example,
.BR "tacctl user add jsmith superuser" ).
.TP
.BI "user remove " name
Remove a user after confirmation.
.TP
.B passwd
Change your own password. Takes no arguments: the target is the tacctl
user whose name matches the login that invoked
.BR sudo (8).
The current password must verify before the new one is accepted, and the
new one is held to the same strength policy as
.BR "user passwd" .
Available to every tier.
.TP
.BI "user passwd " "name " [ "--hash " hash ]
Change a user's password. Prompts for the new password with
confirmation. If
.BI --hash " hash"
is given, the bcrypt hash is used directly and no password is prompted
(mirrors
.B user add --hash
for users who self-generate via
.BR "tacctl hash commands" ).
.TP
.BI "user disable " name
Disable a user while preserving the password hash for later re-enable.
.TP
.BI "user enable " name
Re-enable a previously disabled user.
.TP
.BI "user rename " "old new"
Rename a user.
.TP
.BI "user move " "name group"
Move a user to a different group without changing the password.
.TP
.BI "user verify " name
Show user details and verify the password interactively. A stored hash
with a bcrypt cost above 16 (tacctl writes 10\-14) is refused before the
password is asked, since checking it would take hours; tacquito still
authenticates it.
.TP
.B "user scope " \fIname\fR " " [ "list " | " add " \fIs\fR[,s...] | " remove " \fIs\fR[,s...] | " replace " \fIs\fR[,s...] | " remove \-\-all" ]
Manage which scopes
.I name
can authenticate from. This is a membership list: a user with no scopes
can authenticate nowhere. With no subcommand (or
.BR list ),
prints the user's current scopes with orphan detection (any scope
name that does not resolve to an existing scope is flagged red).
.B add
and
.B remove
accept one or a comma-separated list of existing scope names;
.B replace
replaces the whole list;
.B "remove \-\-all"
empties it (with confirmation) and takes no scope names.
.B "remove \-\-all"
preserves the bcrypt hash — distinct from
.B user disable
in that intent is "temporarily revoke scope access" rather than
"revoke the password".
.SS Group management \fB(tacctl group)\fR
.TP
.B group list
List all groups with Cisco privilege level, Juniper class, and user count.
.TP
.BI "group show " name
Every setting of the group and where it comes from (set on the group, or
the default of its priv-lvl): Cisco priv-lvl, Juniper class, tacctl tier,
WTI level, command rules, Junos rules and the number of users.
.TP
.B "group add " \fIname priv-lvl juniper-class\fR " [ --tier \fItier\fR ] [ --wti-level \fIlevel\fR ]"
Add a custom group.
.I priv-lvl
is an integer 0\(en15;
.I juniper-class
matches the Junos login class name.
.B --tier
and
.B --wti-level
set those two settings at creation (see
.BR "group edit" ).
.TP
.B "group edit " \fIname\fR " { priv-lvl \fIN\fR | juniper-class \fICLASS\fR | wti-level \fIlevel\fR | tier \fItier\fR }"
Change one setting of a group. Every group, built-in or custom, is edited
the same way; the built-in groups
.RB ( readonly ,
.BR operator ,
.BR superuser )
can be edited but not removed.
.B wti-level
is
.BR viewonly ,
.BR user ,
.B superuser
or
.BR administrator ,
the access level a WTI unit with Service Name
.B wti
gives the group's users;
.B tier
is
.BR readonly ,
.BR operator ,
.B engineer
or
.BR superuser ,
the group's tacctl tier on the server and on Linux hosts.
.B auto
removes either setting, so the priv-lvl decides again (below 7 readonly,
7\(en14 operator, 15 superuser).
.TP
.BI "group remove " name
Remove a custom group. Built-in groups are protected.
.TP
.B "group commands list " \fIgroup\fR
Show the per-command authorization rules for a group, in the order
tacquito tries them (the
.B #
column), including the
default action (the action of the trailing
.RB ` * `
catchall rule).
.TP
.B "group commands default " \fIgroup\fR " { permit | deny }"
Set the default action for unmatched commands. Stored as the action
of a trailing
.RB ` * `
rule that tacquito's
.B CommandBasedAuthorizer
matches as a wildcard.
.TP
.B "group commands add " \fIgroup\fR " " \fIname\fR " [ --match \fIregex\fR ]... [ --action permit | deny ] [ --before \fIname\fR | --first ]"
Add a rule before the
.RB ` * `
catchall, or with
.B --before
before the first rule of that name, or with
.B --first
at position 1. Default action is
.BR permit .
The
.I name
is matched against the TACACS+
.B cmd=
field literally. Each
.B --match
adds a regex that tacquito tests against the command's
.I arguments
only: the space-joined
.B cmd-arg
values after the command word, without
.B <cr>
(auto-anchored with
.RB ` ^…$ `,
so
.B ^crypto
matches
.B show crypto
only; write
.B "^crypto( .*)?"
to cover the arguments after it too).
For
.B show running-config
the string tested is
.BR running-config ,
never the full line, so a regex that repeats the command word
.RB ( "^show .*$" )
can never match and is rejected. A regex may not contain a comma (the
rule's stored line form separates matches with commas); write
.B \ex2c
for one. Omit
.B --match
to cover any arguments.
The first matching rule wins; tacquito returns FAIL if no rule
matches and the
.RB ` * `
catchall is not
.BR permit .
.TP
.B "group commands remove " \fIgroup\fR " " \fIname\fR " [ --match \fIregex\fR ]... [ --action permit | deny ] [ --all ]"
Drop a rule by name. When several rules share the name it refuses
unless
.B --match
(the rule's regexes, all of them, in order) and
.B --action
narrow them to one, or
.B --all
drops them all. The
.RB ` * `
catchall is protected; change it via
.B group commands default
or remove the entire block via
.B group commands clear .
.TP
.BI "group commands clear " group
Remove a group's command-rule override from
.IR tacctl.yaml .
The merged view falls back to the shipped defaults for built-in groups
.RB ( readonly ,
.BR operator ,
.BR superuser );
for custom groups the group ends up with no rules at all.
.I tacquito.yaml
is regenerated afterwards so device-side enforcement stays in sync.
Confirmation required.
.TP
.B "group commands seed " [ \fIgroup\fR ] " " [ --force ]
Recovery tool. Writes the legacy broader rule set (with
.BR enable ,
.BR clear ,
.BR test ,
.BR monitor ,
etc.) as overrides in
.IR tacctl.yaml .
On fresh installs the smaller shipped defaults (viewable via
.BR "tacctl config defaults" )
are already in effect; you only need
.B seed
if you've customized and want to start from a known, broader baseline.
Without an argument, seeds all three built-ins; with a group name, only
that one (custom groups are not seeded). Refuses to overwrite existing
rules without
.BR --force .
Legacy seed defaults:
.B readonly
permits
.IR "show ping traceroute terminal exit end quit logout who where enable"
with default deny;
.B operator
adds
.IR "clear test monitor"
to the readonly set with default deny;
.B superuser
gets a
.RB ` "* permit" `
catchall.
.TP
.BI "group privilege list " group
Show the Cisco
.B "privilege exec level"
mappings for a group, each with its mode (exec, exec all, configure,
configure all) in a column. Reports whether the source is an explicit
override (in
.I /etc/tacctl/tacctl.yaml
under
.BR privileges.\fIgroup\fR )
or the built-in default set (move-DOWN commands only).
.TP
.B "group privilege add " \fIgroup\fR " '\fIcmd\fR'[,'\fIcmd\fR'...]"
Move one or more Cisco commands to the group's priv-lvl. Quote
multi-word commands (e.g.
.RB ' "show running-config" ' );
separate multiple commands with commas. An entry may start with a mode:
.B exec:
(the default when there is none),
.BR "exec all:" ,
.B configure:
or
.BR "configure all:" ,
e.g.
.RB ' "configure: router bgp" ',' "exec all: show ip" ';
.B config cisco
renders it as
.BR "privilege " \fImode\fR " [all] level " \fIN\fR " " \fIcmd\fR .
An entry is stored in its mode's form, so
.B "exec: show ip"
and
.B "show ip"
are the same mapping. On first
.B add
the group's defaults are inherited; subsequent adds extend that set.
Every command is validated before anything is written — a bad command
aborts the whole op.
.TP
.B "group privilege remove " \fIgroup\fR " '\fIcmd\fR'[,'\fIcmd\fR'...]"
Drop one or more previously-mapped commands. Accepts the same
comma-separated list form as
.BR add .
If only defaults are in effect, the remove takes effect against the
default set (suppressing those commands for that group).
.TP
.BI "group privilege clear " group
Wipe explicit mappings for a group; the built-in default set takes
over again.
.TP
.B "group privilege seed " [ \fIgroup\fR ] " " [ --force ]
Populate the built-in groups with their default mapping set. Same
shape as
.BR "group commands seed" .
Default sets:
.B readonly
none (priv-1 is the floor);
.B operator
ships a priv-15 read/diagnostic family moved DOWN to priv-7
.RI ( "show running-config" ,
.IR "show startup-config" ,
.IR "show tech-support" ,
.IR "show archive" ,
.IR "show access-list" ,
.IR "show ip route" );
.B superuser
none (priv-15 is the ceiling).
.PP
Cisco devices enforce these rules LIVE over TACACS+. Once any group has rules,
.B tacctl config cisco
emits
.B aaa authorization commands \fIlevel\fB default group TACACS-GROUP local
for every privilege level a group uses, and
.BR "aaa authorization config-commands" ,
and IOS asks tacquito for every command. A level where some group has no
rules gets the line commented out, naming the group and the fix
.RB ( "group commands default \fIgroup\fB permit" ),
since the server would deny that group every command. Junos devices do
not use these rules: the server sends them each group's own deny sets
.RB ( "group junos" ).
RADIUS
has no per-command authorization: the server does not enforce these rules
for logins over RADIUS
.RB ( status ,
.B "config render"
and
.B "backend enable"
name the groups concerned).
.TP
.B "group junos " \fIgroup\fR " list"
Both Junos deny sets of the group, with their sizes.
.TP
.B "group junos " \fIgroup\fR " { deny-commands | deny-configuration } { list | add | remove | clear } [ '\fIregex\fR' ]"
Show, add to, remove from or drop one set. Each pattern is a POSIX
extended regular expression Junos tests against the whole command line
.RB ( deny-commands )
or the configuration path
.RB ( deny-configuration ).
The set is sent at login as one value, the patterns joined with
.BR | ,
over TACACS+ (junos-exec) and RADIUS (Juniper VSAs); it may not exceed
241 bytes (deny-commands) or 236 (deny-configuration), the TACACS+
argument limit, and a pattern that would make it longer is refused.
.B deny-commands
does not cover
.BR "show configuration" \fIpath\fR:
put paths in
.BR deny-configuration ,
which also hides them from reading. Sets live under
.BI junos. group
in tacctl.yaml.
.TP
.B "group junos " \fIgroup\fR " clear"
Drop both sets (confirms).
.TP
.B "group preset roles [ --dry-run ] [ --force ] [ --mgmt-filter \fIname\fR ]"
Starting values for four roles: viewer (the built-in
.BR readonly ),
.BR operator ,
.B engineer
(created at priv-lvl 15 with class ENG-CLASS when absent; tier
.BR engineer )
and
.BR superuser :
their WTI levels, the Junos deny sets of the first three and engineer's
Cisco command rules (everything but the device's trust boundary and
lifecycle). It lists every setting as set, unchanged, or kept (a
different value is there; pass
.B --force
to replace it), then asks before writing.
.B --dry-run
writes nothing;
.B --mgmt-filter
adds the Junos firewall filter guarding the management interface to
engineer's deny-configuration. Users are not moved
.RB ( "user move" ).
Every value can be changed afterwards with
.BR "group edit" ,
.B "group junos"
and
.BR "group commands" .
.SS Server configuration \fB(tacctl config)\fR
.TP
.B config show
Show the current configuration summary. Each enabled backend adds its config
file, service state and the listeners it should have, probed on the port
(and protocol) of the listener, not on port 49; with more than one backend
enabled each has a labelled block.
.TP
.B config dump
Print the tacctl tunable posture: canonical defaults
(built into tacctl),
operator overrides
.RI ( tacctl.yaml ),
and the effective merged view.
.TP
.B config defaults
Print the canonical defaults YAML only (no overrides, no merge).
Useful for seeding a new
.I tacctl.yaml
or piping into
.B diff
against an existing one.
.TP
.BI "config get " "path" " [fallback]"
Read one dotted-path value (e.g.
.BR bcrypt.cost )
from the merged view. Emits empty + the fallback if the path is missing.
Lists and maps produce no output; use
.B "config get-list"
or
.B "config dump"
instead.
.TP
.BI "config get-list " "path"
Read a list value (e.g.
.BR mgmt_acl.permits )
from the merged view, one element per stdout line.
.TP
.B config validate
Validate the store
.RI ( store.yaml :
names, every user's group and scopes exist, each prefix owned by one scope),
YAML syntax for
.I tacquito.yaml
and
.IR tacctl.yaml ,
schema-walk every override key in
.I tacctl.yaml
against the write-time validator (catches hand-edits that bypass the
setter commands — including malformed
.BR commands.\fIgroup\fR
rules,
.BR privileges.\fIgroup\fR
lists, out-of-range integers, unknown keys), then check the server config
for structural issues: missing sections, orphan scope refs, default-scope
marker pointing at a nonexistent scope, per-scope key divergence,
reserved usernames
.RB ( root ,
.BR tacquito )
present as tacctl-managed users, users missing the
.B accounter:
field, and per-scope key divergence.
.IP
Then, for every enabled backend, whether its rendered config is up to date
with the store and whether any of its files was edited by hand (drift). With
more than one backend enabled each gets a labelled block, and a drifted file of
one backend does not hide another's state; files left behind by a disabled
backend are not checked. With RADIUS enabled it warns about a scope RADIUS
serves that sends no vendor attribute and is not a Linux-host scope. Exits 1
when it finds errors.
.TP
.BR "config render " [ \-\-force ]
.TQ
.BI "config render \-\-dry\-run \-\-out " dir
Render every enabled backend's config (for TACACS+:
.I tacquito.yaml
and the systemd drop-ins) from the store and
.IR tacctl.yaml ,
all backends or none, and restart the ones whose files changed. Needed after
hand-restoring files or when
.B config validate
reports a config that is missing or out of date. Refuses to overwrite an
artifact that was edited by hand (drift); with
.B \-\-force
it replaces it and keeps a copy under
.IR /etc/tacctl/backups/legacy/ .
.IP
.B \-\-dry\-run
renders every enabled backend exactly as a render stages it (the daemon's own
configuration check included) and writes the artifacts under
.I dir
at their live paths
.RI ( dir /etc/tacquito/tacquito.yaml ,
and so on) instead of installing them. Nothing live is touched: no artifact,
no render record, no service. A hand edit does not stop a dry run.
.I dir
must be new or empty and is private to root, since the artifacts hold secrets
and password hashes.
.TP
.BI "config diff " [ timestamp ]
Alias of
.BR "backup diff" .
.TP
.BI "config restore " "timestamp " [ \-\-legacy ]
Alias of
.BR "backup restore" .
.TP
.BI "config loglevel " [ debug | info | error ]
Show or change the TACACS+ server's log level. Stored in
.I tacctl.yaml
.RB ( backends.tacacs.level ),
so the change survives upgrades.
.TP
.B "config listen " "[ \-\-backend \fIid\fR ] [ \-\-listener \fIname\fR ] { show | tcp | tcp6 | udp | udp6 | reset } " [ address ]
Show, change, or reset a listen address (default: the
.B tacacs
backend's listener
.BR default ;
the backend ids are listed by
.BR "backend list" ).
Stored in
.I tacctl.yaml
.RB ( listeners. \fIbackend\fB. \fIname\fR),
from which tacctl renders the TACACS+ units' systemd drop-ins and the
RADIUS
.B listen
sections.
.B reset
clears the override and falls back to the built-in default.
With
.BI \-\-listener " name"
the command addresses another TACACS+ listener, which runs as its own unit
.BI tacquito@ name .service
with its own accounting log; there
.B reset
removes it. TACACS+ listeners take
.B tcp
or
.BR tcp6 ;
RADIUS ones
.RB ( "\-\-backend radius" )
take
.B udp
or
.B udp6
and are named: the built-in
.B auth
(udp :1812) and
.B acct
(udp :1813); a further name is an authentication listener. The unit is
restarted and must stay up, or
.I tacctl.yaml
and the drop-ins are put back. A listener's
.B tls
block in
.I tacctl.yaml
is reserved:
.B "tls.enabled: true"
is rejected (TACACS+ over TLS is not implemented).
.B
Switching to tcp6
prompts for confirmation because dual-stack sockets accept IPv4
clients via mapped addresses
.RB ( ::ffff:x.x.x.x )
that silently bypass IPv4 prefix ACLs; see
.B NOTES.
.TP
.B "config metrics " "{ show | enable | disable | address " \fIhost:port\fR " | reset }"
Control tacquito's prometheus HTTP exporter's listen address (TACACS+ only;
FreeRADIUS has no exporter). Stored as
.BR backends.tacacs.metrics_address .
.B show
(no arg) prints current state and address.
.B enable
clears the override and reverts to the unit default
.RB ( 127.0.0.1:8080 ,
loopback-only).
.B disable
sinks the exporter to
.B 127.0.0.1:0
— an ephemeral loopback port that no scraper can discover, so the
exporter is effectively off even though its goroutine still runs. This
indirection is deliberate: tacquito's upstream
.B -export-promhttp=false
path unconditionally cancels the server context, which would tear the
whole daemon down, so tacctl avoids the flag and uses an unreachable
bind instead.
.B "address " \fIhost:port\fR
binds explicitly (e.g.
.B 10.1.0.1:8080
to expose to external scrapers); non-loopback binds print a warning.
.B reset
clears the override and falls back to the unit default.
.TP
.B "host list"
Show enrolled Linux hosts with their SSH target, scope, server address,
method and user count.
.TP
.B "host show " name " [ \-\-all ] [ \-\-json ] [ \-\-check ]"
One enrolled host in full, in sections:
.B Connection
(target, port, identity file, marked
.B (missing)
when it is gone, server address and method;
.B "this server"
for a host enrolled with
.BR \-\-local ),
.B Scope
(the registered scope, the scope and prefix that answer the host's address
today, the warning
.B "host list"
gives when they differ, with its
.BI "host move " name
fix, and a staging /32 still pending for it),
.B Address
(the recorded address, the previous one and when it changed),
.B "Host keys"
(the pinned keys' SHA256 fingerprints and the command that shows them on the
host),
.B Sightings
(last and first seen, by which user over which backend, the NAS-Identifier),
.B Notices
(the open ones with their remedies and a count of the acknowledged ones;
.B \-\-all
lists those too, marked),
.B Accounts
(the tacctl users the next sync makes there: name, tier, UID and groups;
disabled users counted, not listed),
.B "Last sync"
(when, by whom, the result or the reason it failed, the client script
protocol, and the accounts it created, updated and removed) and
.B "Host facts"
(the OS from /etc/os-release, sshd's version, the PAM module with its version,
and useradd's UID range from /etc/login.defs, as that sync read them over its
session). The last two come from
.IR /etc/tacctl/hosts/ name .json ,
written by every
.B "host enroll"
and
.B "host sync"
(a failed sync too) and removed by
.BR "host unenroll" ;
a host not synced since 0.2.2 shows
.BR "not recorded (before 0.2.2)" .
They are only shown, never used to decide anything.
.B \-\-json
prints every field.
.B \-\-check
also logs in read-only, as
.B "host target"
tests a login (sudo with a password needs a terminal, else it fails with the
message
.B "host sync"
gives), and compares the host with what tacctl would make it: tacctl's groups
and their GIDs, each account's UID, primary group and home mode, the PAM files
tacctl writes (present, and the text the client script recorded writing),
the client script protocol it last ran, and the host's ssh keys against the
pins. Nothing is changed. Each difference is one line with the command that
fixes it (usually
.BI "host sync " name ),
and the exit status is 1 when there is one. Same tier as
.BR "host list" .
.TP
.B "host enroll " "{ [" user "@]" host " | \-\-local } [ \-\-method tacplus|radius ] [ \-\-scope " name " ] [ \-\-server " address " ] [ \-\-name " name " ] [ \-\-port " n " ] [ \-\-identity " file " ] [ \-\-build\-on\-host ] [ \-\-allow\-uid\-mismatch ] [ \-\-remove\-home ]"
Copy the
.B "config linux"
install script to a host over SSH, run it there as root, and register the
host. The method is
.B tacplus
(pam_tacplus against the TACACS+ backend) or
.B radius
(the distribution's pam_radius_auth package against the RADIUS backend:
.B libpam\-radius\-auth
on Debian and Ubuntu,
.B pam_radius
from EPEL on the RHEL family, where the script enables EPEL when the host
does not have it, and stops before any account or PAM change when it cannot
install the package). It is, in order:
.BR \-\-method ;
the method the host is registered with; the scope's
.BR "scope auth-method" ;
the one protocol the scope's
.B protocols
filter names;
.B "host default-method"
(default
.BR tacplus ).
The method's backend must be enabled and the scope must allow its
protocol. Re-enrolling a registered host with the other method switches it:
the first method's module, secret file and SELinux module are removed
(packages stay). Without
.B \-\-scope
a registered host keeps the scope it is registered in, and any other host
goes into the scope that answers its address (said, with the prefix); a
host whose address no scope covers is refused before anything changes,
with the commands that add the address to a scope or give the host a scope
of its own
.RB ( "scope add linux\-" "name " "\-\-prefixes " addr "/32 \-\-secret generate" ,
so a secret read off one host is useless from another). Naming another
scope for a registered host moves it: enroll first names the users whose
accounts the move deletes and asks on a terminal; without one it stops
unless given
.BR \-\-yes .
tacctl never moves a host itself:
.BR "host sync" ,
.B "host list"
and
.B "config validate"
warn, with the enroll that moves it, when another scope answers the
address recorded for a host. The scope must be the
one that answers the host's requests (the first prefix that holds the
address they come from): for
.B \-\-local
that address is 127.0.0.1, and a scope that does not cover it is refused
before anything changes; for another host, a scope that does not cover the
address its name resolves to is a warning (NAT may change it).
.B "host sync"
of this machine warns when its scope no longer covers 127.0.0.1.
The server address
defaults to the source address of the route to the host.
.B \-\-local
enrolls this machine without SSH. ssh runs as the user who invoked
.BR sudo (8),
using their keys; the remote login must be root or able to sudo. That login
(the provisioning account:
.I user
of the target, else the invoking user's name) must be a local account on the
host that does not authenticate through tacctl, so enrolment and sync keep
working when this server cannot be reached;
.B "host enroll"
refuses a user of tacctl, and
.B "host sync"
warns about an existing enrolment that uses one.
.B "tacctl ssh"
never uses it. The
remote copy of the script is deleted after the run. Nothing is registered
if the remote run fails. Accounts are created, expired and deleted as by
.B "host sync"
(including the question about removed users' homes, and
.BR \-\-remove\-home ).
.IP
After a successful run the host's public key files are read over the same
ssh connection
.RB ( "cat /etc/ssh/ssh_host_*_key.pub" ,
read-only; the connection is the administrator's own ssh, checked against
their known_hosts), and the target is scanned
.RB ( ssh\-keyscan
of the host and port, as root). Only the keys both reads hold are pinned in
the device registry for
.BR "tacctl ssh" ;
the output lists their
.B SHA256:
fingerprints. A key type the two reads give different keys for pins nothing:
both sets are printed and
.B "host hostkey\-mismatch"
is logged to syslog (auth.warning). A type only one read has is reported and
not pinned. Key files that cannot be read, or a host that does not answer the
scan, pin nothing, with a warning and the
.B "device hostkey"
command; the enrolment succeeds either way.
.B "host sync"
of a pinned host compares the key files with the pin and reports a
difference; it never re-pins. A host enrolled with
.B \-\-local
is not pinned.
.IP
For
.BR tacplus ,
pam_tacplus is built on this server, in a rootless
.BR podman (1)
container matching the host's OS release, cached, and shipped as a binary;
Debian, Ubuntu and RHEL-family hosts (RHEL, AlmaLinux, Rocky, CentOS
Stream, Oracle Linux) are supported, the latter built against AlmaLinux of
the same major release;
the host compiles it from source instead when no image matches its OS, its
architecture differs, the container build fails, or
.B \-\-build\-on\-host
is given. A host that already has the module from the same source is not
touched. Requires
.B "config linux build"
to have been run. For
.B radius
none of this applies
.RB ( \-\-build\-on\-host
warns that it is not applicable). Both methods are tested on Ubuntu 24.04,
Debian 12 and 13, AlmaLinux and Rocky Linux 8, 9 and 10; SELinux in
enforcing mode is not verified.
.IP
A reject from the server is final. When the server does not answer (or the
host's shared secret is wrong), login falls through to the local password,
which tacctl's accounts do not have; local administrators and every other
local account outside
.B tac\-users
never reach the server. With the server silent a user of tacctl is refused
after about 5\(en6 s (tacplus) or 8\(en9 s (radius).
pam_radius_auth 2.0.x on Ubuntu 24.04 and Debian 12 neither sends nor checks
Message-Authenticator (BlastRADIUS, CVE-2024-3596); elsewhere tacctl adds
.BR require_message_authenticator .
Session accounting over RADIUS is sent only when the RADIUS
.B acct
listener is on the authentication port plus one, and never from Ubuntu
24.04 (malformed records).
.TP
.B "host move " "{ " name " [ " scope " ] | \-\-all } [ \-\-yes ] [ \-\-remove\-home ] [ \-\-allow\-uid\-mismatch ]"
Move an enrolled host to another scope: an enroll with its registered
target, port, identity, server and method, into
.I scope
or, without one, the scope that answers its address. Like an enroll naming
the scope, it names the users whose accounts the move deletes and asks
first (without a terminal it stops unless given
.BR \-\-yes ).
.B \-\-all
moves every host another scope answers.
.TP
.B "host sync " "{ " name " | \-\-all } [ \-\-allow\-uid\-mismatch ] [ \-\-remove\-home ]"
Push the scope's current user list to the host: new users get accounts,
tier changes move group membership, disabled users' accounts are expired
(files kept; enabling the user restores the account), and the accounts of
users no longer in the scope (or no longer tacctl users) are deleted with
.BR userdel (8),
with an own group an earlier release made when it is empty. Their UIDs stay
reserved on the server. Run after
.BR "user add" ", " "user remove" ", " "user move" ", " "user disable" " or " "user scope"
changes.
.IP
tacctl's groups have fixed GIDs, the first numbers of the range:
.B tac\-users
(+0, every host: every managed account's primary group and the PAM gate),
.B tac\-console
(+1, the tacctl server only: sshd's console drop-in),
.B tac\-superuser
(+2, every host: sudo),
.B tac\-operator
(+3) and
.B tac\-readonly
(+4) (the tacctl server only: its tiers sudoers). Accounts have no group
of their own and get a 0700 home. A sync moves what an earlier release made:
primary groups to
.BR tac\-users ;
own groups removed when empty; groups moved to their fixed GIDs
.RB ( "groupmod \-g" );
on a host other than the server, the tier and console groups removed when
only tacctl's accounts are in them. A fixed GID another group holds is left
to it, with a warning.
.IP
tacctl manages an account only when it created it on that host and its UID
there is in the server's range (80000\(en89999 by default; see
.BR "config linux uid\-range" ). Any other account is never created, expired,
deleted or otherwise changed, with one exception: it is taken out of
tacctl's own groups
.RB ( tac\-users ", " tac\-readonly ", " tac\-operator ", " tac\-superuser ", " tac\-console )
and nothing else on it changes, so it is a plain local account. A local
account named like a user of tacctl that tacctl did not create is otherwise
left as it is, and that user gets no account on that host (a warning says
so); the rest of the sync goes on. The summary counts the accounts tacctl
manages on the host and names the refused users, as in
.RB \(lq "c1: synced (4 users; 1 refused: carl)." \(rq.
.IP
An account tacctl created with a UID of a range the server used before
(20000\(en29999, where releases up to 0.2.0 gave them out, or the range before a
.BR "config linux uid\-range" )
is renumbered first, to the same offset in the current range: its UID
.RB ( "usermod \-u" ,
which re-owns the home directory tree)
.RI "(\(lq'" user "': renumbered " old " \-> " new " (home re\-owned)\(rq;"
the summary counts them, as in
.RB \(lq "c1: synced (3 users; 1 renumbered)." \(rq).
Files under
.IR /home ", " /tmp ", " /var/tmp ", " /var/spool/cron " and " /var/mail
that still carry the old number are listed (at most 20), never changed. An
account whose user is logged in, or whose new UID is taken on the
host, is left as it is and named as refused until a later sync.
.IP
A removed user's home directory is deleted only when asked for. From a
terminal, enroll and sync read the host's
.B "getent passwd"
over the same ssh connection (read-only, before anything runs there) and ask
.RI "\(lqDelete /home/" user " of removed user '" user "'? [y/N]\(rq"
for each removed user;
.B \-\-remove\-home
deletes them all without asking; with no terminal and no flag every home is
kept. A kept home is moved to
.IR /home/.tacctl\-removed/ user \- YYYYmmdd \- HHMMSS
(the directory is root's, mode 0700; the moved tree is made root:root with
.BR "chown \-hR" ,
never following a link), so a local account that later gets the same UID
does not inherit it; the host prints
.RI "\(lqhome kept: /home/.tacctl\-removed/" user \- time \(rq.
A home that is not a directory directly under
.IR /home ,
is a symbolic link, is not owned by the account, is also another
account's home or is on another file system is neither deleted nor moved
.RI "(\(lqhome kept in place: " home " (" reason ")\(rq)."
.IP
Enroll and sync also read the host's
.I /etc/login.defs
(read-only) and warn when its
.BR UID_MIN \(en UID_MAX
range overlaps the server's range, where local
.BR useradd (8)
could give out a UID tacctl has assigned (the default
.B "UID_MAX 60000"
does not overlap 80000\(en89999); keep it clear of the range there. tacctl
never edits the file. Before anything runs there, enroll and sync read the
host's
.I /proc/self/uid_map
and
.IR gid_map ,
and refuse a host whose user namespace cannot hold the range
.RI "(\(lq'" host "' cannot hold UIDs " min \- max ": its user namespace maps only " ranges " (an unprivileged container). \&...\(rq)."
.IP
Enroll and sync record the address the ssh connection reached (the host's
side of
.BR SSH_CONNECTION ;
.B \-\-local
records 127.0.0.1) as the host's address in the device registry:
.B "device add"
refuses it, sightings from it are the host's, and
.B "device discover"
does not list it. A sync that finds another address records it, logs
.BR "host address\-changed" ,
and raises the
.B address\-changed
notice
.RB ( "device notice " \fIname\fP " ack address\-changed" ).
.IP
A host with no pinned host key gets the keys it offers pinned, as at
enrollment. A pin is never changed by a sync: a key that differs from the
pinned one is reported, with the pinned and offered fingerprints, and new key
types are reported; both are fixed with
.BI "device hostkey " name " accept" \fR.
.TP
.B "host target " name " [ " "\fR[\fPuser@\fR]\fPhost" " ] [ \-\-port " n " ] [ \-\-identity " file " | \-\-no\-identity ]"
With only a name, show how tacctl reaches the enrolled host: its ssh target,
port, identity file, the server address the host uses, the address recorded
for it, its scope and method. With a new target, port or identity
(administrators only; not for a host enrolled with
.BR \-\-local ),
test the change first: log in as
.B "host enroll"
and
.B "host sync"
do (the invoking user's ssh, agent, port and key), check that the login is
root or may use sudo (sudo that asks for a password is refused without a
terminal), read the host's ssh keys over that session and compare them with
the pinned ones (another key refuses the change, with both fingerprint sets:
the host must be the same machine), and record the address reached. A
provisioning login that is a user of tacctl is refused, as at enrollment. Only
then is the registry line rewritten (scope, server and method kept), after a
snapshot, and logged
.RB ( "host target name= target= port= by=" ).
No script runs on the host.
.TP
.B "host unenroll " name " [ \-\-force ]"
Run the removal script on the host and drop it from the registry, with its
pinned host keys. Local accounts and home directories are left in place:
the removal script changes no account. If the removal fails the
host stays registered unless
.B \-\-force
is given.
.TP
.BR "host default-method " [ tacplus | radius ]
Show or set the method for hosts enrolled without
.B \-\-method
whose scope decides none
.RB ( host.default_method
in
.IR tacctl.yaml ;
default
.BR tacplus ).
Warns when the method's backend is not enabled.
.TP
.B "config linux build"
Fetch the pinned
.B pam_tacplus
release (v1.7.0, verified by commit hash) and prepare a self-contained
source tarball under
.IR /var/lib/tacctl/linux .
Needs autoconf, automake, libtool, gnulib and the PAM headers on the
server. Run once before generating
.B tacplus
scripts (the
.B radius
method needs nothing built).
.TP
.B "config linux script " "[ \-\-scope " name " ] [ \-\-server " address " ] [ \-\-method tacplus|radius ] [ \-\-output " file " ]"
Write an install script that sets up TACACS+ or RADIUS login on a Debian,
Ubuntu or RHEL-family host in the given scope (method as for
.BR "host enroll" ,
without the registered host). The script embeds the scope's shared secret (it
is written mode 0600; delete it after use), for
.B tacplus
the source tarball, and the
scope's users with their tier and a stable UID. Run as root on the target
it installs the module (tacplus: builds it; radius: installs the
distribution's package), creates a local account per user (locked password)
in the groups
.BR tac-users " and " tac-readonly ", " tac-operator " or " tac-superuser ,
points the
.BR sshd ", " sudo ", " sudo\-i ", " login ", " sddm " and " gdm\-password
PAM services (where present) at the server for members of
.B tac-users
only, and grants
.B tac-superuser
full sudo. Accounts of removed users that it created are deleted (homes
only with
.BR \-\-remove\-home );
a local account named like a user that it did not create is left alone and
that user gets no account. Other local accounts are not affected. It refuses
to run when no local administrator with a usable password exists outside
the TACACS+ user list, and puts the PAM files back if any step fails.
Re-run it with
.B \-\-accounts\-only
to sync users after changes on the server.
.TP
.B "config linux uid " "[ " username " [ " uid " ] ]"
List the UID assigned to each user, print one user's UID, or change it
(only a change writes the file).
Each user has one number, its UID on every enrolled host (its primary
group is
.BR tac\-users ); it is assigned the first time the user is sent to a host,
from the server's range only (see
.BR "config linux uid-range" ;
after the highest one given, never reused, not even after the user is
removed), and kept in
.IR /etc/tacctl/linux\-uids ,
whose first line records the range it is numbered for.
A value outside that range is refused; an entry outside it from an earlier
release is listed as not used on hosts, and its user gets no account until
given a number in the range. When the range is used up, the script refuses
the next user.
The install and sync scripts stop before changing anything if that number
is already used on a host by another account, and print the ways to
resolve it: renumber the conflicting account on the host, assign the
user a different number with this command, or accept another free number
of the range on that host with
.BR \-\-allow\-uid\-mismatch .
Changing a UID does not renumber accounts that already exist on hosts; a
difference from the assigned one is reported on every sync.
.TP
.B "config linux uid\-range " "[ " min \- max " ]"
Show or change the range Linux host accounts get their UIDs from (its
first five numbers are also the GIDs of tacctl's groups): one range for all hosts, kept in
.I tacctl.yaml
as
.B linux.uid_min
and
.BR linux.uid_max ,
80000\(en89999 by default. The default lies above the distributions'
.BR useradd (8)
range (UID_MAX 60000) and systemd's reserved UIDs, inside the range systemd
leaves unused (65536\(en524287), and below the usual
.I /etc/subuid
start (100000). A range is refused when it holds fewer than 1000 numbers,
starts below 1000, ends above 4294967293, or overlaps what systemd reserves
(60001\(en60513, 61184\(en65519, 65534\(en65535, 524288 and up); one that
overlaps useradd's default 1000\(en60000 is accepted with a warning (every
host's
.I /etc/login.defs
must then keep clear of it).
A change renumbers
.I linux\-uids
at once by offset (the first number of the old range becomes the first of
the new one; the old file is kept as
.IR linux\-uids.pre\-renumber\- time ),
and every host renumbers the accounts tacctl created there at its next
enroll or sync. It is refused, changing nothing, when an entry would land
past the new range's end, when a number would be another name's, or when
the new range overlaps one the file was numbered for before (unless it
starts at the same number: a range grown or shrunk at its end moves
nothing). A file numbered by a release that gave out UIDs from 20000 up
(no record, entries in 20000\(en29999) is renumbered the same way by the
first
.BR "config linux uid" ", " "config linux script" ", " "host enroll" " or " "host sync" .
A host that is an unprivileged container whose user namespace does not map
the whole range (rootless podman or LXC usually map 0\(en65535) is refused
by
.B "host enroll"
and
.BR "host sync" ;
give it an ID map that covers the range, run it privileged, or choose a
range every host can hold.
.TP
.B "config linux builds " "[ list | clear ]"
List or delete the pam_tacplus modules that
.B "host enroll"
built in containers, one per OS release and architecture, kept in
.IR /var/lib/tacctl/linux/builds .
The listing shows the digest of the base image each was built from.
.TP
.B "config linux remove-script " "[ \-\-output " file " ]"
Write a script that removes TACACS+ or RADIUS login from a host (either
method): it undoes the PAM edits, deletes the files holding the shared
secret, the module, the SELinux module and the sudoers drop-in, and reports
accounts left with no way to log in. Local accounts, home directories, the
tac-* groups and installed packages are left in place.
.TP
.B "config snmp show"
The SNMP access
.B "device add"
and
.B "device check"
read a device's
.B sysName.0
with: the version, port, timeout and v3 protocols, and whether the community
and the v3 user and passphrases are set (never what they are). The settings are
.B snmp.version
.RB ( v2c " or " v3 ;
unset: no lookup),
.B snmp.port
(default 161),
.B snmp.timeout
(seconds, 1 to 10, default 2; one retry),
.B snmp.v3.auth
.RB ( sha ,
the default, or
.BR sha256 )
and
.B snmp.v3.priv
.RB ( aes128 )
in
.IR tacctl.yaml ;
the credentials are in
.IR /etc/tacctl/snmp.yaml .
v3 is authPriv only (HMAC-SHA-96 or HMAC-SHA-256-192, AES-128-CFB), with
engine discovery. Administrators only: the credentials reach every device.
.B "tacctl config snmp"
with no subcommand prints the usage.
.TP
.B "config snmp community " "[ \-\-stdin ]"
Ask for the v2c community twice, without echo (or read one line from stdin
with
.BR \-\-stdin ),
store it, and set
.B snmp.version
to
.BR v2c .
.TP
.BI "config snmp v3\-user " user " [ \-\-auth sha|sha256 ] [ \-\-priv aes128 ] [ \-\-stdin ]"
Store the v3 user with its authentication and privacy passphrases (each asked
twice without echo, or one line each from stdin with
.BR \-\-stdin ;
at least 8 characters), and set
.B snmp.version
to
.B v3
and the protocols given.
.TP
.BI "config snmp port " "[ n ]"
Show or set the agents' UDP port.
.TP
.BI "config snmp timeout " "[ seconds ]"
Show or set the wait for an answer, 1 to 10 seconds (one retry after it).
.TP
.B "config snmp clear"
Remove the credentials and unset
.BR snmp.version :
no lookups.
.TP
.BI "config snmp test " address|device
Read the sysName of one address (or registered device) and print it, or the
reason there is none: no answer (on v2c a wrong community, and on v3 a wrong
privacy passphrase, look the same: the agent drops the request), or the v3
report the agent sent
.RB ( unknownUserName ,
.BR wrongDigest ,
\&...). Exit status 1 without a name.
.TP
.B "config sudoers " "{ show | install | remove } " [ group ]
Manage the optional
.I /etc/sudoers.d/tacctl
drop-in that grants NOPASSWD sudo on
.B tacctl
to a group (default:
.BR adm ).
Installs are validated with
.BR visudo (8)
before taking effect. Not installed by default.
.TP
.B "config sudoers tiers " "{ show | install | remove }"
Manage
.IR /etc/sudoers.d/tacctl-tiers ,
which grants tacctl by tier to tacctl users that have local accounts.
Members of
.B tac-readonly
may run
.BR passwd ", " status ", " version ", " help " (also " \-h ", " \-\-help "), " "user list" ", " "user show" ", "
.BR "group list" ", " "scope list" ", " "backend list" ", " "backend status" ", "
.BR "device list" ", " "device show" ", " "device notices" ", " "device ssh\-config" ", "
.BR "ssh " \fIname\fR " and " "device ssh " \fIname\fR
(the devices of their own scopes only), and the console's own
.BR _console\-policy .
Members of
.B tac-operator
may additionally run
.BR "log tail" ", " "log search" ", " "log failures" ", " "log accounting" ", "
.BR "config validate" ", " "backup list" ", " "device check" ", " "device scan" ", "
.BR "device discover" ", " "device export" " and " "console show" .
Members of
.B tac-superuser
get full
.BR sudo (8)
access. Lower-tier rules are NOPASSWD. Commands that print a shared
secret or a password hash are superuser-only. tacctl re-checks the
caller's tier itself on every run, reading the tier from the user's group
in the store
(priv-lvl 15 superuser, 7\-14 operator, below 7 read-only); callers
outside the local group
.B tac-users
are not tier-restricted.
.B "tacctl upgrade"
rewrites an installed
.I /etc/sudoers.d/tacctl\-tiers
that differs from the release's rules (after
.B visudo \-cf
accepts them), so a release's new verbs reach the tiers; it never creates the
file. Both sudoers files carry
.B Defaults!/usr/local/bin/tacctl env_keep += "SSH_AUTH_SOCK TACCTL_CONSOLE DISPLAY"
(the agent socket for
.BR "tacctl host" ,
the login console's session marker, and the X11 display
.B "tacctl ssh \-X"
hands on) and nothing else of the caller's environment; tacctl also refuses a
.B SUDO_USER
that is not the account of
.BR SUDO_UID .
.TP
.BI "config password-age " [ days ]
Show or set the password age warning threshold. Default 90 days.
.TP
.BI "config bcrypt-cost " [ "10-14" ]
Show or set the bcrypt cost factor used when hashing new or changed
passwords. Default 12. Existing hashes keep their minted cost.
.TP
.BI "config password-min-length " [ "8-64" ]
Show or set the minimum length enforced by
.B validate_password_strength
on interactively-entered passwords. Default 12 (OWASP 2025). Floor 8
(NIST 800-63). Auto-generated passwords (operator accepts the blank
prompt) bypass this check.
.TP
.BI "config secret-min-length " [ "16-128" ]
Show or set the minimum length enforced when an operator types a
custom shared secret via
.BR "scope secret " \fIname\fR " set" .
Default 16 (Cisco TACACS+ best practice). Auto-generated secrets
bypass this check.
.TP
.B "config allow " "{ list | add | remove | clear } " [ cidr[,cidr...] ]
Manage the connection allow list (IP ACL).
.B add
and
.B remove
accept a single CIDR or a comma-separated list.
.B clear
wipes the entire list (with confirmation) — the result fails open so
any source IP becomes eligible to connect, subject to the deny list
and the secret-provider prefixes.
.TP
.B "config deny " "{ list | add | remove | clear } " [ cidr[,cidr...] ]
Manage the connection deny list. Same comma-list and
.B clear
semantics as
.BR "config allow" .
.B deny
takes precedence over
.BR allow ;
with both empty, all connections are accepted.
.TP
.B "config mgmt-acl " "{ list | add | remove | clear | cisco-name | juniper-name } " [ arg ]
Manage a shared CIDR permit list stored under
.B mgmt_acl.permits
in
.I /etc/tacctl/tacctl.yaml
that drives both the Cisco VTY-ACL and a commented Juniper lo0
firewall-filter example emitted by
.B config cisco
and
.BR "config juniper" .
Preserved across
.BR "tacctl upgrade" .
.B list
/
.B add
/
.B remove
/
.B clear
manage the permit list;
.B add
and
.B remove
accept a single CIDR or a comma-separated list.
.B cisco-name
and
.B juniper-name
configure the emitted ACL/filter names (see below). With no arguments,
prints usage, the current entry count, and both ACL names.
.TP
.BI "config mgmt-acl cisco-name " [ name ]
Show or set the name of the IOS access-list emitted by
.BR "config cisco" .
Default
.BR VTY-ACL .
Names must start with a letter and contain only letters, digits,
.RB ` _ ` ,
or
.RB ` - `
(max 63 chars).
.TP
.BI "config mgmt-acl juniper-name " [ name ]
Show or set the name of the Junos firewall filter emitted by
.BR "config juniper" .
Default
.BR MGMT-ACL .
Same character rules as
.BR cisco-name .
Both overrides live under
.B mgmt_acl.names.{cisco,juniper}
in
.I /etc/tacctl/tacctl.yaml
and survive
.BR "tacctl upgrade" .
.TP
.BI "config cisco " [ "--scope " name "] [--legacy] [--protocol " tacacs | radius ]
Print a ready-to-paste Cisco IOS/IOS-XE configuration with the
server IP, shared secret, current group-to-privilege mappings, and
the VTY-ACL built from
.B config mgmt-acl
pre-filled. Over TACACS+ it has one
.B aaa accounting commands
line per privilege level a group uses and, once any group has command
rules, the per-level
.B aaa authorization commands
lines (see
.BR "group commands" ),
.B aaa authorization config-commands
and a commented
.B aaa authorization console
line to uncomment for authorization on the console line. Without
.BR --scope ,
emits the default scope's secret (header names the scope and lists
sibling scopes as a hint). With
.BR --legacy ,
emits legacy IOS 12.x syntax
.RB ( "tacacs-server host" " and " "aaa group server \&... / server <ip>" )
for devices predating the structured
.B "tacacs server"
block introduced in IOS 15.0.
.IP
With
.B "--protocol radius"
(default: the scope's auth-method, see
.BR "scope auth-method" ,
else the one protocol its
.B protocols
filter names, else
.BR tacacs )
the configuration is for the RADIUS backend instead (see
.B RADIUS DEVICE CONFIGURATIONS
below);
.B --legacy
is TACACS+ only.
.TP
.BI "config juniper " [ "--scope " name "] [--protocol " tacacs | radius ]
Print a ready-to-paste Juniper Junos configuration with template
users, TACACS+ server setup, verification commands, and a commented
lo0 firewall-filter example built from
.B config mgmt-acl
pre-filled. The classes carry permission bits only (a class named
.B ENG-CLASS
gets the engineer bits). Step 3 is a read-only summary of what the
server sends per class at login: the group's
.B deny-commands
and
.B deny-configuration
values (from
.BR "group junos" )
with their sizes, or none; nothing in it is pasted. The
.B "group commands"
rules are not translated into class rules. Scope handling matches
.BR "config cisco" .
.B "--protocol radius"
renders the RADIUS configuration (see
.B RADIUS DEVICE CONFIGURATIONS
below).
.TP
.BI "config wti " [ "--scope " name "] [--protocol " tacacs | radius ]
Print a step-by-step procedure for the serial text interface of a WTI
console server (DSM/CPM/REM/TSM/RSM, firmware v8.x): the
.B /N
Network Parameters menu, the TACACS Parameters item, and the value to
key in for every field (server IP, Secret Word, port 49, Fallback Local
derived from the scope's
.BR aaa-order ,
Default User Access
.B On
with Access Level ViewOnly (required: with it Off the unit's OpenSSH
treats TACACS-only users as invalid and sends a junk password),
Account/Session Management Modules, Service Name
.BR wti ,
the factory default, which per-group WTI levels need),
an IP Tables check (the unit must accept ESTABLISHED,RELATED traffic or
tacquito's replies are dropped),
followed by the group-to-access-level mapping, a troubleshooting step
that turns on the unit's TACACS Debug echo, and the matching tacquito
log lines to look for. WTI reads the standard
.B priv-lvl
attribute from the answer to its
.B wti
service: a group with a WTI level of its own
.RB ( "group edit \fIgroup\fB wti-level" )
gets that level, any other group its priv-lvl band (0\-4 ViewOnly, 5\-9
User, 10\-14 SuperUser, 15 Administrator) through the
default-service-permit patch
.RI ( patches/0001 ).
A unit set to Service Name
.B shell
by an earlier walkthrough ignores the WTI levels set on groups until it
is set back to
.BR wti .
The unit's
accounting (Session Management Module) needs tacquito built with the
.I patches/0002
overlay, which sends an empty server_msg on accounting success; the
unit drops the SSH session when it receives upstream's message. Warns when the
scope secret or a scope member's username is unlikely to survive the
unit's menu entry (whitespace, punctuation, over 32 characters). Scope
handling and the protocol (flag, auth-method, the scope's only protocol,
TACACS+) match
.BR "config cisco" .
.IP
Over RADIUS the walkthrough is for the unit's RADIUS Parameters menu
.RB ( /N ,
item 29 in WTI's user guide) and prints the group to
.B WTI-Super
table (0 ViewOnly, 1 User, 2 SuperUser, 3 Administrator, in the bands
above). It has
.B not
been verified on a unit: it is written from WTI's documents
.RI ( docs/radius-notes.md ),
which disagree on the level a login gets when the reply carries no
.BR WTI-Super .
Refused, as for Cisco and Juniper, when the scope sends no
.B WTI-Super
(see
.BR "scope vendor-attrs" ).
.TP
.BI "config branch " [ name ]
Show or change the tacctl repository branch used by
.B tacctl upgrade.
.SS Auth backends \fB(tacctl backend)\fR
A backend is a daemon that serves the store over one protocol:
.B tacacs
(TACACS+ served by tacquito, installed by
.BR install )
and
.B radius
(RADIUS served by FreeRADIUS, opt-in); which are enabled is
.B backends.enabled
in
.I tacctl.yaml
(default:
.BR tacacs ).
See
.BR BACKENDS .
.TP
.B backend list
Every backend this tacctl has: protocol, implementation, whether it is
installed, whether it is enabled, and its service state. Read-only.
.TP
.BI "backend status " [ id ]
Service and listener state of every backend (or one): state, uptime, PID,
and for each listener its network and address, the state of its unit and
whether something is listening there. Read-only.
.TP
.BI "backend enable " "id " [ \-y ]
Enable a backend. If it is not installed, confirms (\fB\-y\fR skips) and runs its
install steps (packages, service account, unit). Then, as one step: every
backend that will be enabled has its gate checked (a hand-edited rendered file
refuses the command, exit 3), a snapshot is taken, the id is added to
.BR backends.enabled ,
and the config of every enabled backend is rendered, all or none. Then the
service is enabled at boot and started, and must report
.BR active .
If any step fails, the files tacctl owns
.RB ( tacctl.yaml ,
the store, every rendered file) are left or put back exactly as they were, the
service is stopped and disabled, and the exit status is 1; software that the
install step put on the machine stays.
.TP
.BI "backend disable " "id " [ \-y ]
Take a backend out of
.BR backends.enabled ,
stop its service and disable it at boot. Asks for confirmation (\fB\-y\fR
skips) because clients of that protocol stop authenticating. Its package and
the files it rendered stay where they are (and are no longer rendered or
reported as drift);
.B "backend enable"
brings it back. Refused for the last enabled backend (nothing would serve the
store: enable another first, or use
.BR uninstall ),
and for
.B tacacs
on an install that has no store yet (legacy read-only mode, where
.I tacquito.yaml
is still the source of truth).
.IP
Commands need the store
.RI ( /etc/tacctl/store.yaml ),
or, on an install from before the store, its
.IR tacquito.yaml ;
with neither they stop with \(lqConfig not found\(rq.
.I tacquito.yaml
is the
.B tacacs
backend's file, rendered from the store: a RADIUS-only install has none
and runs every command without it. With
.B tacacs
enabled and
.I tacquito.yaml
missing, commands run and warn;
.B "tacctl config render"
(or any change) writes it again.
.PP
.B backend list
and
.B backend status
are available to the read-only and operator tiers;
.B enable
and
.B disable
are superuser only.
.SS Canonical store \fB(tacctl store)\fR
.TP
.BR "store show " [ \-\-json ]
Print the model (users, groups, scopes, filters) as YAML or JSON.
.B Superuser only:
the output contains shared secrets and password hashes.
.TP
.B "store import " "[ \-\-check | \-\-force ] [ \-\-replace ] [ " file " ]"
Import an old-style
.I tacquito.yaml
(default: the live one) into the store.
.B \-\-check
writes nothing and reports what would happen and whether the rendered config
is equivalent;
.B \-\-force
drops content the store cannot represent (each item is listed);
.B \-\-replace
overwrites an existing store. Also the way to keep a hand edit of
.I tacquito.yaml
that
.B config validate
reports as drift.
.TP
.B store rollback
Undo the move into the store: restore the pre-store
.IR tacquito.yaml ,
remove the store, restart. The install is back in legacy read-only mode.
.SS Log inspection \fB(tacctl log)\fR
With more than one backend enabled each log subcommand shows every backend's
log in a section headed by its name; with one, the output is unchanged.
.B \-\-backend
.I id
(any subcommand, anywhere on the line) limits it to one backend. TACACS+
logs to the journal of its units and to
.IR /var/log/tacquito/accounting*.log ;
RADIUS to
.I tacctl\-auth.log
(one line per Access-Accept or Access-Reject, with scope, client, NAS and
reason),
.I tacctl\-accounting.log
and
.I tacctl\-radius.log
in the package's log directory.
.TP
.BI "log tail " "[ \-f | \-\-follow ] [ " n " ]"
Show the last
.I n
entries (default 20): the tacquito journal; the RADIUS auth log and daemon log.
With
.BR \-f ,
then print new entries of every backend as they arrive (each line behind
its backend's id when more than one is enabled) until Ctrl-C.
.TP
.BI "log search " term
Search logs for a username or keyword (TACACS+: over the last 7 days).
.TP
.B log failures
Show authentication failures from the last 24 hours.
.TP
.BI "log accounting " [ n ]
Show the last
.I n
accounting records
.RI ( /var/log/tacquito/accounting.log ;
the RADIUS detail file).
.TP
.B "log clear " [ --force ]
Purge tacquito journal entries
.RB ( "journalctl --rotate && journalctl --vacuum-time=1s -u tacquito" )
and truncate the TACACS+ accounting logs; truncate the RADIUS auth,
accounting and daemon logs.
Prompts for confirmation;
.B --force
(or
.BR -y )
skips the prompt for scripted use. Destructive — historical auth and
accounting records are irrecoverable once cleared.
.SS Backup management \fB(tacctl backup)\fR
.TP
.B backup list
List snapshots, newest first, then old-style backups.
.TP
.BI "backup diff " [ timestamp ]
Diff
.I store.yaml
and
.I tacctl.yaml
against a snapshot (default: most recent). Superuser only: the output
shows shared secrets and password hashes.
.TP
.BI "backup restore " timestamp " " [ \-\-legacy ]
Restore both files from a snapshot after confirmation, re-render every
enabled backend's config (all or none), and restart the backends. The current
state is snapshotted first, so a restore can itself be undone. Nothing changes
if the snapshot is invalid or the render fails. A snapshot's
.B backends.enabled
comes back with the rest of
.IR tacctl.yaml :
a backend it enables must already be installed (otherwise nothing changes and
the message says to run
.BR "backend enable" ),
is enabled at boot and started; one it leaves out is stopped and disabled. With
.B \-\-legacy
the argument names an old-style
.I tacquito.yaml.<timestamp>
file, which goes through
.B store import \-\-check
before it is imported.
.PP
A snapshot is the directory
.I /etc/tacctl/backups/<timestamp>/
holding
.IR store.yaml ,
.I tacctl.yaml
and a manifest (the
.I rendered.json
of that moment and the tacctl version), root-only. One is taken
automatically before every change, unless the files already equal the
newest snapshot. The newest 30 are retained. Without a store, list, diff
and restore work on old-style backups only.
.SS Hash generation \fB(tacctl hash)\fR
.TP
.B hash
With no subcommand (or with
.BR help ),
prints a usage summary listing
.B generate
and
.BR commands .
Does not require root.
.TP
.B hash generate
Interactively prompt for a password on the server and print its bcrypt
hash. Intended for admins who can shell in to the tacquito host.
.TP
.B hash commands
Print client-side bcrypt-generation recipes for operators who cannot
SSH to the server. Covers Linux/macOS (Python + htpasswd), Windows
(Python + PowerShell/BCrypt.Net), and any-OS Node.js. Each recipe
prints both the raw
.B $2b$\&...
and hex-encoded forms;
.B "user add --hash"
/
.B "user passwd --hash"
accept either and normalize to hex on write.
.SS Interactive shell \fB(tacctl shell)\fR
.TP
.BI "shell " "[ \-\-no\-history ] [ \-\-idle " min " ] [ \-c " line " ]"
A prompt,
.BR "tacctl> " ,
where each line is a tacctl command without the
.B tacctl
in front:
.BR "user list" ,
.BR "scope show lab" .
The shell runs as the invoking user and holds no privilege: each line runs as
.B sudo
.RB [ \-n ]
.I tacctl words
with the terminal attached, so sudo's policy, its log line and tacctl's tier
gate apply to every line exactly as from a login shell, and prompts, password
input and Ctrl-C work as they do there.
.B \-n
is used for members of
.B tac-users
in the readonly or operator tier, or in no tier group (they have no local
password and their rules are NOPASSWD); a line that sudo refuses them and
that their tier's sudoers rules do not cover is reported as not permitted for
the tier. A member of
.B tac-superuser
runs plain
.BR sudo ,
which asks for the network password on the terminal when a line needs it
(once; sudo's cache then applies), so superusers' write verbs work from the
shell.
.B hash
and
.B completion
lines run without sudo.
For
.B host
lines the agent socket
.RB ( SSH_AUTH_SOCK )
is passed as it is when tacctl re-executes itself under sudo.
Messages and exit statuses are those of the command; after a command that
ends with a non-zero status the shell prints
.BR "[exit " \fIN\fB ] .
.IP
A line is split into words at blanks (space, tab); single quotes, double
quotes and backslash quote as in
.BR sh (1),
and nothing else is special: there are no variables, globbing, pipes,
redirections, command separators, command substitution or history expansion
(\c
.BR ; ,
.BR | ,
.BR $( ,
backticks,
.BR > ,
.B !
are ordinary characters of a word). A line is at most 4096 bytes.
The shell's own words are
.B help
(the top-level usage),
.BI "help " command
(the usage of that command's family),
.B history
(the lines entered, as stored),
.B exit
and
.BR quit .
.IP
Editing keys: Left/Right, Home/End (Ctrl-A, Ctrl-E), Esc-b and Esc-f (or
Alt-Left, Alt-Right) by word, Backspace, Delete, Ctrl-K, Ctrl-U, Ctrl-W,
Ctrl-T, Ctrl-L; Up/Down (Ctrl-P, Ctrl-N) step through the history and
Ctrl-R searches it backwards as you type (Ctrl-R again: further back; Enter
runs the match; Ctrl-G gives the line back). Tab completes commands,
sub-commands, flags and names (users, groups, scopes, hosts, devices, ...,
asked of
.B sudo \-n tacctl _completion-names
and kept for 5 seconds); a second Tab lists the matching words alone, in
columns that fit the terminal. ? inserts nothing and shows help for the
cursor's position: where words can come, \(lqPossible completions:\(rq with
each one's argument column and description (a device or host with its
vendor, address and scope); after a command that takes arguments, its usage
lines, its options and what comes next (\(lqNext: <username> <group>\(rq,
\(lqNext: <Enter> to run\(rq). Inside quotes, or after a backslash, ? is
typed as a character. A list of more than 40 entries asks first
(\(lqShow all 45 devices? [y/N]\(rq): y shows it, any other key (or
Ctrl-C) prints how to narrow it and gives the line back. Help from ? taller
than the terminal is shown a screenful at a time (\(lq\-\- more (Space:
page, Enter: line, q: quit) \-\-\(rq; q or Ctrl-C stops). A paste is inserted as one line (its newlines become
blanks, at most 4096 bytes) and runs only on Enter. Ctrl-C at the prompt discards the
line; Ctrl-C while a command runs ends the command, and the shell carries on.
Ctrl-Z and Ctrl-\e do nothing: there is no job control, and the commands
inherit that. Ctrl-D on an empty line, like
.BR exit ,
leaves the shell.
.IP
The history is kept in
.I ~/.local/state/tacctl/history
(mode 0600, the newest 1000 lines).
A line is stored redacted: everything after
.BR \-\-secret ,
.B \-\-hash
or
.BR \-\-password ,
after
.B set
in a
.B secret ... set
line, after
.B import
when a value follows it, and after the word of a
.B passwd
line is replaced by
.BR \[u2026](redacted) ;
.B "scope secret lab set x"
is stored as
.BR "scope secret lab set \[u2026](redacted)" .
A recalled line that carries the mark is refused; type the value again.
Lines ended with Ctrl-C are not stored.
.B \-\-no\-history
keeps the history of the session in memory only.
.IP
.BI "\-\-idle " min
ends the shell after
.I min
minutes at the prompt without input (0, the default: never; at most 1440);
the time a command runs does not count.
.IP
.BI "\-c " line
runs one line and exits with its status; the line is stored in the history.
With standard input not a terminal (\c
.BR "tacctl shell < file" ),
the shell runs the lines of the input in order, with no prompt and no
history, and stops at the first line that ends with a non-zero status,
exiting with that status. The commands of such a batch get no standard input
(it is the script), so a command that asks a question cannot be answered
from it: use its
.B \-y
flag where it has one.
Over ssh, ask for a terminal to get the interactive shell:
.BR "ssh \-t " "host tacctl shell" ;
without
.B \-t
ssh allocates none, and the shell reads commands from standard input and
says so on standard error.
Bare
.B tacctl
on a terminal still prints the usage and exits 1.
.SH SCOPE MANAGEMENT
A
.I scope
is a named bundle of (client CIDR prefixes, shared secret), kept in the
store and shared by every backend: the same prefixes and secret serve
TACACS+ and RADIUS, unless the scope's
.B protocols
filter limits it. Each user's
.B scopes
list references one or more of these by name. A client is matched to the
scope with the most specific prefix that contains its address (tacquito
walks its rendered
.B secrets:
list, sorted by specificity, first match wins; FreeRADIUS takes the longest
prefix); authentication succeeds only when that scope is in the user's
list.
.PP
Fresh installs seed a single scope named
.B lab
(least-privilege default). Operators add additional scopes (for
example
.BR prod ,
.BR edge ,
.BR dmz )
as needed, and place users into them explicitly.
.SS Top-level \fB(tacctl scope)\fR
.TP
.B scope list
One row per scope (deduplicated), with each scope's prefixes joined
as a comma list. Handy for a high-level inventory — names, prefix
counts, user counts, and default marker. For the first-match routing
view (one row per scope-prefix pair, numbered by slice position,
mirroring how tacquito routes a connecting client) use
.BR "scope routing" .
For full per-scope detail (secret posture, per-scope knobs, user list) use
.BR "scope show " \fIname\fR .
.TP
.B scope routing
Print every (scope, prefix) pair in first-match order, as rendered into
tacquito's
.B secrets[]
list (one row per scope-prefix pair,
numbered by slice position). Multi-prefix scopes repeat their name on
each prefix row so the display literally mirrors routing priority —
reading top to bottom shows the order tacquito tries providers for a
connecting client.
.TP
.BI "scope show " name
Full detail for one scope: prefixes, users that reference it, the
shared secret's length and posture (never its value:
.BI "scope secret " name " show"
prints that), whether the scope is
the default, its protocols filter and auth-method, its vendor attributes
and tagged addresses (RADIUS).
.TP
.B "scope add " \fIname\fR " --prefixes " \fIcidr\fR[,cidr...] " [ --secret \fIvalue\fR | --secret generate ] [ --protocols \fIcsv\fR ] [ --vendor-attrs \fIcsv\fR ] [ --default ]
Create a new scope. Prefixes are required; each must not already
belong to another scope (overlap with a broader scope's supernet is
fine — the new scope's narrower prefix will win first-match).
.B --secret generate
produces a strong key automatically.
.B --default
makes the new scope the default for
.BR "user add" .
.B --protocols
and
.B --vendor-attrs
set what
.B "scope protocols set"
and
.B "scope vendor-attrs enable"
would.
.TP
.BI "scope remove " "name " [ --force ]
Remove a scope from the store (and so from every backend's rendered
config). Refuses by default if any user references the
scope; with
.BR --force ,
strips the name from all affected users AND removes the scope,
with a loud warning. Its vendor attributes and tagged addresses go with it. Every
.I tacctl.yaml
setting stored under the scope's name
.RB ( aaa.order ,
.BR exec_timeout ,
.BR tacacs_group ,
.BR radius_group ,
.BR scope_auth_method ,
.B scope_mgmt_acl.names.cisco|juniper
and
.BR scope_mgmt_acl.permits )
is removed with it, so a new scope of that name starts from the defaults.
.TP
.BI "scope rename " "old new"
Rename a scope. Rewrites the scope in the store (protocols, vendor
attributes and tagged addresses included), every user's
.B scopes
reference, and
.B scope.default
in
.I tacctl.yaml
(if it pointed at
.IR old ),
and moves every
.I tacctl.yaml
setting stored under the old name to the new one.
.TP
.BI "scope default " [ name ]
Show (no arg) or set (with arg) the default scope. Stored under
.B scope.default
in
.IR /etc/tacctl/tacctl.yaml .
Used by
.BR "user add"
when
.B --scopes
is omitted, and by
.BR "config cisco" " / " "config juniper" " / " "config wti"
when
.B --scope
is omitted.
.TP
.BI "scope lookup " ip\fR|\fIcidr
Resolve an IPv4 / IPv6 address or CIDR to the scope that owns it,
following the same slice-order walk tacquito's provider selector uses.
Prints the matching prefix and, if multiple scopes' prefixes cover the
query (e.g. a supernet/subnet overlap), lists the shadowed scopes
below the winner for debugging. An address the scope has tagged with a
vendor
.RB ( "scope devices" )
is shown with its tag.
.SS Per-scope prefixes \fB(tacctl scope prefixes)\fR
.TP
.BI "scope prefixes " "scope " "list"
Show the scope's current prefixes.
.TP
.B "scope prefixes " \fIscope\fR " add " \fIcidr\fR[,cidr...]
Add one or more CIDRs. Each CIDR is canonicalized
.RB ( 10.1.5.5/24 " -> " 10.1.5.0/24 ),
checked against the one-CIDR-per-scope invariant (rejected with the
owning-scope name if already claimed elsewhere), and appended as its
own
.B secrets[]
entry in the rendered
.IR tacquito.yaml ,
where every entry is sorted globally by prefix
specificity so tacquito's slice-ordered first-match walk routes each
client to the narrowest scope that contains it (and its own RADIUS client).
.TP
.B "scope prefixes " \fIscope\fR " remove " \fIcidr\fR[,cidr...]
Remove one or more CIDRs. The last prefix of a scope cannot be removed
(use
.B "remove \-\-all"
or
.BR "scope remove" ),
and a prefix whose removal would leave a tagged address
.RB ( "scope devices" )
outside the scope is refused.
.TP
.BI "scope prefixes " "scope " "remove \-\-all " [ \-\-force ]
Empty the entire prefix list for the scope (with confirmation): the scope
and its per-scope settings are removed, so it vanishes from every rendered
config. The command refuses to proceed when users reference the scope
unless
.B \-\-force
is given, which strips the scope from those users.
.B \-\-all
takes no CIDRs, and
.B \-\-force
is only valid with it. A scope enrolled Linux hosts use is not removed
(move them first:
.BR "host move" ).
.TP
.B "scope prefixes " \fIscope\fR " move " \fIcidr\fR[,cidr...] " " \fIother\fR
Move the CIDRs from
.I scope
to
.I other
in one change, so no address they hold is left unanswered. The same checks
as remove and add apply. Enrolled hosts whose addresses another scope then
answers are named, with the
.B "host move"
that follows them; none is moved.
.SS Staging addresses \fB(tacctl scope staging)\fR
.TP
.BI "scope staging " "[ list | remove " address " ]"
A host or network device provisioned off-site for a scope, at a bench
address the scope does not cover
.RB ( "host enroll ... \-\-scope " "scope " "\-\-staging" ,
.BR "config cisco" | juniper | wti " \-\-scope " "scope " "\-\-staging " "ip " [ "\-\-name " device ]),
has that address added to the scope as a /32: the scope's own secret and
users answer it there, so nothing changes on the device when it is
installed in the scope's prefixes. The /32 is removed once the host (its
enroll or sync) or the registered device
.RB ( "device set" )
is seen at another address the scope covers.
.B list
(the default) shows them with where each is seen now;
.B remove
takes one out by hand.
.SS Per-scope secret \fB(tacctl scope secret)\fR
.TP
.BI "scope secret " "scope " "show"
Print the scope's raw shared-secret value plus its length and posture.
The command runs as root, the store is mode 0600, and operators need
the raw value to paste into device configs — so
.B show
prints it rather than hiding it. Superuser only.
.TP
.BI "scope secret " "scope " "set " value
Set the scope's key after validation (≥
.B config secret-min-length
chars, mixed character classes). With RADIUS enabled, a secret FreeRADIUS
cannot carry (a backslash together with a
.BR $ )
fails the render and is refused;
.B "config render"
warns about one beyond the interoperability advice (63 printable
characters, no space). The secret is shared by TACACS+ and RADIUS.
.TP
.BI "scope secret " "scope " "generate"
Generate a strong key with
.B openssl rand -base64 24
and apply it.
.SS Per-scope protocols \fB(tacctl scope protocols)\fR
.TP
.BI "scope protocols " "scope " "[list]"
Show which protocols serve the scope. Without a filter, every enabled
backend serves it.
.TP
.BI "scope protocols " "scope " "set " protocol\fR[,protocol...]
Limit the scope to the named protocols
.RB ( tacacs ", " radius ).
A backend whose protocol is not listed leaves the scope out of what it
renders: for TACACS+ that means its prefixes, its secret and its users'
grant of it are absent from tacquito.yaml; for RADIUS, its prefixes are not
clients. Refused when the list leaves out the scope's
.BR "scope auth-method" .
.TP
.BI "scope protocols " "scope " "clear"
Remove the filter.
.SS Per-scope vendor attributes (RADIUS) \fB(tacctl scope vendor-attrs, tacctl scope devices)\fR
Over RADIUS an Access-Accept always carries
.B Service-Type
.RB ( Administrative-User
at priv-lvl 15, else
.BR NAS-Prompt-User ).
A vendor's privilege attribute is added only where the operator opted in:
.B Cisco-AVPair \(dqshell:priv-lvl=N\(dq
(cisco),
.B Juniper-Local-User-Name
(juniper),
.B WTI-Super
0\-3 (wti). An Access-Reject carries none. TACACS+ is not affected.
.TP
.BI "scope vendor-attrs " scope
Show the vendors enabled for the scope, or
.B "not sent"
(the state of a new scope).
.TP
.BI "scope vendor-attrs " scope " enable|disable " vendor\fR[,vendor...]
Enable or disable a vendor's attribute for every device of the scope that
is not tagged. There is no
.BR set ,
.B clear
or
.BR none :
nothing is sent until it is enabled. Stored on the scope in
.I store.yaml
.RB ( vendor_attrs ),
so the change re-renders the RADIUS backend and restarts it.
.TP
.BI "scope devices " scope " [list]"
List the scope's tagged addresses.
.TP
.BI "scope devices " scope " set " "ip\fR|\fIcidr vendor"
Tag an address (a bare IP is a /32 or /128) or a range of the scope
with its vendor: it gets that vendor's attribute and no other, whatever
the scope enables. The address must be one
.B "scope lookup"
answers with this scope: inside a prefix of it, and inside no more
specific prefix of another scope. Stored on the scope in
.I store.yaml
.RB ( devices ).
.TP
.BI "scope devices " scope " unset " ip\fR|\fIcidr
Remove a tag.
.B "scope prefixes remove"
refuses to leave a tag outside the scope's prefixes (unset it first, or
add the new prefix before removing the old one); tags go with the scope
on rename and remove.
.SS Per-scope idle-session timeout \fB(tacctl scope exec-timeout)\fR
.TP
.BI "scope exec-timeout " scope " [" minutes ]
Show or set the idle-session timeout (in minutes) that
.B "config cisco --scope " \fIscope\fR
and
.B "config juniper --scope " \fIscope\fR
render into device configs. Cisco emits
.BR "exec-timeout \fIn\fR 0"
on
.B line con 0
and
.BR "line vty 0 15" ;
Junos emits
.BR "set system login idle-timeout \fIn\fR" .
Range 0..60 (Junos's max). A value of
.B 0
disables idle expiry on both vendors. Default 60.
.SS Per-scope mgmt-acl \fB(tacctl scope mgmt-acl)\fR
.TP
.BI "scope mgmt-acl " scope " " \
"<list|add|remove|clear|cisco-name|juniper-name>" " [" args "]"
Per-scope management ACL. Subcommand shape mirrors the global
.B "tacctl config mgmt-acl"
so operators who know one form know the other.
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
.BR list ,
.BR "add " <cidr>[,...] ,
.BR "remove " <cidr>[,...] ,
.B clear
— manage the per-scope permit list. Render fallback is per-scope
-> global
.RB ( mgmt_acl.permits )
-> empty. Empty per-scope lists prune the override so the scope
falls back to the global; `clear` is the explicit way to revert.
.IP \(bu 2
.BR "cisco-name " [ label ] ,
.BR "juniper-name " [ label ]
— per-scope ACL / filter name. Fallback is per-scope -> global
.RB ( "config mgmt-acl cisco-name / juniper-name" )
-> shipped default (\fBVTY-ACL\fR for Cisco, \fBMGMT-ACL\fR for
Junos). Name must satisfy ACL-name rules (letter-start +
[A-Za-z0-9_-], 1..63 chars).
.RE
.TP
.IP
Cisco puts the effective name in
.B "ip access-list standard <NAME>"
and the matching
.B "access-class <NAME> in"
on
.BR "line vty 0 15" ;
Junos puts it in
.B "set firewall family inet filter <NAME>"
and the commented lo0 apply. Per-scope permits let one environment
deviate without changing the house-wide default.
.SS Per-scope Cisco aaa-group-server label \fB(tacctl scope tacacs-group)\fR
.TP
.BI "scope tacacs-group " scope " [" name ]
Show or set the Cisco
.B aaa group server tacacs+ <NAME>
label for this scope. Rendered into every AAA group definition and
the method-list / accounting lines that reference the group. Per-scope
so each environment can match site naming conventions (e.g.
.BR TACACS_PROD ,
.BR ISE-GROUP ).
Must conform to Cisco ACL naming rules: letter-start, letters, digits,
and
.BR [_-] ;
1..63 chars. Default (unset)
.BR TACACS-GROUP .
Junos has no equivalent — its AAA configuration does not group named
servers this way.
.SS Per-scope Cisco RADIUS group label \fB(tacctl scope radius-group)\fR
.TP
.BI "scope radius-group " scope " [" name ]
The RADIUS twin of
.BR "scope tacacs-group" :
show or set the Cisco
.B aaa group server radius <NAME>
label rendered by
.BR "config cisco --protocol radius" .
Same naming rules (letter-start, letters, digits and
.BR [_-] ;
1..63 chars). Default (unset)
.BR RADIUS-GROUP .
Junos has no equivalent. Stored as
.B radius_group.<scope>
in
.IR tacctl.yaml .
.SS Per-scope default authentication method \fB(tacctl scope auth-method)\fR
.TP
.BI "scope auth-method " scope " [" tacacs | radius | clear ]
Show or set the protocol this scope uses when a command names none:
.BR "config cisco" ,
.B "config juniper"
and
.B "config wti"
without
.BR --protocol ,
.B "host enroll"
(for a host that is not registered yet) and
.B "config linux script"
without
.B --method
.RB ( tacacs
is host method
.BR tacplus ).
The order is: an explicit
.B --protocol
or
.BR --method ;
a registered host's own method; this setting; the one protocol the
scope's
.B protocols
filter names, when it names exactly one; the global default (device
configurations TACACS+, hosts
.BR "host default-method" ).
.B clear
removes the setting
.RB ( default
is accepted for it);
.B tacplus
is accepted for
.BR tacacs ,
which is the one spelling it prints.
Refused when the scope's
.B protocols
filter leaves the protocol out;
.B "scope protocols"
in turn refuses a list without the scope's auth-method.
.B "config cisco --legacy"
on a scope that resolves to RADIUS needs
.BR "--protocol tacacs" .
Stored as
.B scope_auth_method.<scope>
in
.IR tacctl.yaml ;
follows
.B "scope rename"
and is removed with the scope.
.SS Per-scope AAA method-list order \fB(tacctl scope aaa-order)\fR
.TP
.BI "scope aaa-order " scope " [" tacacs-first | local-first ]
Show or set the order of methods in the Cisco
.B aaa authentication login
/
.B aaa authorization
lines and Junos
.B system authentication-order
emitted by
.B "config cisco --scope " \fIscope\fR
and
.B "config juniper --scope " \fIscope\fR .
Default (unset)
.B tacacs-first
keeps TACACS+ authoritative (local only kicks in on server outage).
Set
.B local-first
per-scope when a local break-glass account must authenticate even
while TACACS+ is reachable — any local username that collides with a
TACACS+ user will win locally on devices in this scope, so scope
local accounts to emergency / break-glass credentials only.
The change takes effect for the next
.B "config cisco"
/
.B "config juniper"
render of the named scope — existing device configs are unchanged
until the new method-list stanzas are pushed.
.SH DEVICE REGISTRY
The
.I device registry
gives names to the network devices that authenticate against this server, so
that they can be listed, looked up and acknowledged by name. It is kept in
.I /etc/tacctl/devices.yaml
(mode 0600, root only) and never touches
.IR store.yaml :
a device's scope and vendor tag are looked up from the store each time they are
shown, not stored, and registering a device renders nothing and restarts
nothing. Every write takes a snapshot first (see
.BR "backup list" ).
.PP
A device is found by name or by its registered address; names are compared
without regard to case. Enrolled Linux hosts
.RB ( "tacctl host" )
share the namespace: they are listed and shown as
.B linux
devices, read-only, and a device cannot take a host's name (nor
.B "host enroll \-\-name"
a device's). An address is registered at most once. An address that is not
registered is not found, even when a scope covers it.
.PP
A name that is a factory or image default (\c
.BR switch ,
.BR router ,
.BR cisco ,
.BR ubuntu ,
.B ip\-10\-0\-0\-1
and the like; matched without regard to case) is refused with the command that
gives the device a name of its own, unless
.B \-\-allow\-generic
is given; the device then carries a
.B generic-name
notice. A host enrolled under such a name before the registry existed is not
refused, and carries the notice. The built-in list can be extended with
.B generic_names:
(regular expressions matched against the whole name) in
.IR devices.yaml .
.PP
Host keys are pinned when a device is registered: tacctl reads the keys the
device offers with
.BR ssh\-keyscan (1)
(as root, outbound, without logging in), keeps them in
.IR devices.yaml ,
and prints their
.B SHA256:
fingerprints to be compared with the device console. Enrolled hosts are pinned
by
.B "host enroll"
and
.BR "host sync" .
Every registry write regenerates
.IR /var/lib/tacctl/ssh/known_hosts ,
which lists exactly the pinned keys under each device's name, for
.BI "ssh \-o UserKnownHostsFile=/var/lib/tacctl/ssh/known_hosts \-o GlobalKnownHostsFile=none \-o HostKeyAlias=" name " \-o StrictHostKeyChecking=yes" .
A pin changes only through
.BR "device hostkey accept" " or " "device hostkey set" ,
never by itself.
.PP
What the daemons saw of each device is kept in the
.I seen cache
.RI ( /var/lib/tacctl/devices\-seen.json ),
which
.B "device scan"
fills from the logs: the tacquito journal (the
.BI "accepting user [" user "] from [" address "]"
and
.B "failed to validate the user"
lines, the
.B "bad secret detected for ip"
and
.BI "remote [" address "] has no secret providers"
errors, and at log level 30 the
.B "prefix secret provider matches remote"
lines) and FreeRADIUS's
.I tacctl\-auth.log
with its rotations. For each address it keeps the first and last sighting,
the count, the last user, outcome and backend, and the last NAS-Identifier.
.B list
and
.B show
read the cache only, and say how old it is.
.PP
Notices are computed each time they are shown; only acknowledgements are
stored. Besides the registry's own
.RB ( generic-name ,
.BR hostkey-unpinned ,
and for an enrolled host
.BR address\-changed :
its address changed between two enrolments or syncs),
the seen cache raises
.B ambiguous-nas-id
(one NAS-Identifier sent from several addresses; a fully qualified host name
on each device tells them apart),
.B generic-nas-id
(a device identifying itself by a generic name),
.B name-mismatch
(a NAS-Identifier other than the registry name or the hostname, either in full
or by its first label, as
.B sw1
for
.BR sw1.site-a.example ;
informational),
.B duplicate-address
(two entries at one address, or an address identifying as another entry),
.B identity-changed
(the NAS-Identifier an address sends changed: a replaced or reset unit), and
the host-key re-scan of
.B "device scan"
and
.B "device check"
raises
.B hostkey-changed
(no pinned key offered, or another key of a pinned type; it cannot be
acknowledged, only
.B "device hostkey accept"
or
.B set
clears it),
.B hostkey-added
(a new key type, the pins unchanged) and
.B hostkey-unreachable
(no key could be read; with the last good scan). A scan never changes a pin.
Notices appear in the scan output,
.BR "tacctl status" ,
.BR "device list" ,
.B "device notices"
and
.B "device show"
(which counts the acknowledged ones;
.B \-\-all
on either lists them, marked).
.PP
Reading verbs
.RB ( list ,
.BR show ,
.BR notices ,
.BR ssh\-config )
and
.B "tacctl ssh"
are open to the read-only and operator tiers, which see only the devices and
hosts whose scope is one of their own; an entry no scope covers is seen by
administrators only.
.BR export ,
.BR scan ,
.BR discover ,
.B check
and
.B "list \-\-scan" | "\-\-probe"
are operator-level (the scan output's notices are filtered the same way;
.B discover
lists every address). Everything else needs an administrator.
.SS Device registry \fB(tacctl device)\fR
.TP
.B device
With no subcommand (or with
.BR help ),
prints a usage summary and exits 0.
.TP
.BI "device list " "[ \-\-stale ] [ \-\-unconfigured ] [ \-\-scan ] [ \-\-probe ] [ \-\-json ]"
One row per registered device, then per enrolled host: name, address, vendor,
scope, state, last seen, by, via, and the open notices. The state is
.B configured
when a scope's prefixes cover the address (a host: when its scope exists) and
.B unconfigured
otherwise.
.B \-\-unconfigured
lists only those;
.B \-\-stale
lists the devices whose last sighting is older than
.BR stale\-days ,
which are also marked
.BR stale .
The last seen column is the time of the last sighting,
.BI "rejected " time
when the last exchange was a refusal (with
.B "(bad secret)"
or
.B "(no scope)"
when the device's packets could not be read or no scope covers it), and
.B never
for an address no scan has seen; with no seen data at all the columns print
.BR \- .
The footer says when the seen data was read.
.B \-\-scan
runs
.B "device scan"
first.
.B \-\-probe
adds a
.B REACH
column: a TCP connect to each entry's ssh port, 3 seconds, all at once
.RB ( open ,
.BR closed ,
.BR timeout ,
.BR unreachable ).
This server often has no route to the devices' management ports, so a timeout
may be a false alarm.
.TP
.BI "device show " "name|address " [ \-\-all ] " " [ \-\-json ]
One device or host in full: address, hostname, vendor, port, legacy ssh,
description, the scope that answers the address and the prefix that routes it
(and the scopes it shadows), the vendor tag that covers it, the pinned host keys,
the last and first sighting with the sighting count and the NAS-Identifier the
device sends, and the open notices with a count of the acknowledged ones
.RB ( \-\-all
lists them too, marked). It warns when the
vendor tag and the registered vendor disagree.
.TP
.BI "device add " "[ name ] address " "[ \-\-vendor cisco|juniper|wti|other ] [ \-\-hostname " dns " ] [ \-\-port " n " ] [ \-\-description " text " ] [ \-\-legacy\-ssh ] [ \-\-host\-key SHA256:" fingerprint " | \-\-no\-host\-key ] [ \-\-no\-lookup ] [ \-\-allow\-generic ]"
Register a device. A name is letters, digits,
.RB ` . ` ,
.RB ` _ `
and
.RB ` - ` ,
starting with a letter or digit, at most 253 characters, each dotted part at
most 63: a fully qualified host name such as
.B sw1.site-a.example
is a name. The vendor defaults to
.B other
(and only drives the ssh profile; it does not tag the address in the scope).
The address is stored canonically (IPv6 in compressed lower-case form) and may
carry no prefix length. A name or address that is taken, a reserved word (a
tacctl command, \c
.BR local ,
.BR all ),
and a generic name are refused. The host keys the device offers at its address
and ssh port are read with
.B "ssh\-keyscan \-T 5 \-p"
.I port
.B "\-t ed25519,ecdsa,rsa"
(with
.BR \-\-legacy\-ssh ,
the
.B ssh\-rsa
type is asked for by name too) and pinned, and their
.B SHA256:
fingerprints are printed with the command that shows them on the device's
console; compare them before the first connection. With
.BI "\-\-host\-key SHA256:" fingerprint
the device is registered only when it offers a key with that fingerprint, and
only that key is pinned (the other keys it offers are listed, unpinned). With
.B \-\-no\-host\-key
nothing is scanned: the device is registered unpinned and carries a
.B hostkey-unpinned
notice. A device that does not answer the scan, or offers no key matching
.BR \-\-host\-key ,
is refused and nothing is written. The output says which scope, if any, answers
the address.
.IP
Next to the scan,
.B add
reads the device's own name, its SNMP
.B sysName.0
(see
.BR "config snmp" ),
and prints it
.RI ( "The device calls itself '" sysname "' (SNMP sysName)." ).
When it is neither the name given, the
.BR \-\-hostname ,
nor the first label of either (compared without regard to case), a warning
names the fixes; the add goes ahead. A device that does not answer within the
timeout (one retry), an empty sysName, or no SNMP set up is one info line
.RI ( "No SNMP answer from " address "; no name hint." );
when a scan recorded a NAS-Identifier for the address, that is shown instead,
labelled as such. Nothing of it is stored.
.B \-\-no\-lookup
skips it. With no
.IR name ,
the sysName, lowercased, is offered as the name and taken only after a
.B y
at a terminal; without a terminal, without an answer, or when the sysName is
not a valid device name or is a generic one (a WTI unit's Site ID
.BR WTI ),
the add is refused with the usage line. The offered name goes through every
other check of the add.
.TP
.BI "device remove " "name[,name...] | \-\-all " [ \-y ]
Remove devices from the registry, after confirmation (\c
.B \-y
skips it; a closed stdin cancels). Enrolled hosts, scopes and vendor tags are
not touched.
.TP
.BI "device rename " "old new " [ \-\-allow\-generic ]
Rename a device; acknowledgements stay with it.
.TP
.BI "device address " "name " [ address ]
Show or set the address: the AAA identity of the device, stored canonically. It
cannot be cleared and may not be one another device holds. Pinned host keys belong
to the old address and are dropped.
.TP
.BI "device hostname " "name " [ dns | clear ]
Show, set or clear the DNS name (display, and the ssh target when set).
.TP
.BI "device vendor " "name " [ cisco | juniper | wti | other ]
Show or set the vendor;
.B clear
sets
.BR other .
.TP
.BI "device port " "name " [ n | clear ]
Show, set or clear the ssh port (clear is 22).
.TP
.BI "device description " "name " [ text | clear ]
Show, set or clear the description (up to 120 characters).
With no value these verbs print the current one (\c
.B \-
when unset).
.TP
.BI "device legacy\-ssh " "name " "[ enable | disable ]"
Show or set the opt-in for the legacy key-exchange and host-key algorithms
older IOS releases need (off by default).
.TP
.BI "device stale\-days " [ n ]
Show or set the number of days (1 to 3650, default 30) after which a device
without a sighting counts as stale.
.TP
.BI "device notice " "name " "{ ack | unack } " kind
Acknowledge an accepted notice, or reopen it. Acknowledgements are kept per
device and per kind in
.IR devices.yaml ;
acknowledged notices leave
.B list
and
.B notices
and stay in
.B show
marked as acknowledged. The kinds are
.BR generic-name ,
.BR hostkey-unpinned ,
.BR hostkey-added ,
.BR hostkey-unreachable ,
.BR ambiguous-nas-id ,
.BR generic-nas-id ,
.BR name-mismatch ,
.BR duplicate-address ,
.B identity-changed
and
.BR address\-changed ;
.B hostkey-changed
cannot be acknowledged, and an enrolled host acknowledges only
.BR address\-changed .
.TP
.BI "device notices " "" [ name ] " " [ \-\-all ]
The open notices (all devices, or one), each with the command that fixes or
acknowledges it;
.B \-\-all
lists the acknowledged ones too, marked. The notices the registry raises are
.BR generic-name ,
for a device or host whose name is a generic one, with the per-vendor command
that sets a name on the device and the
.B device rename
that follows, and
.BR hostkey-unpinned ,
for a device with no pinned host key, with the console command that shows its
fingerprint and the
.B device hostkey accept
that pins it.
.TP
.BI "device import " "[ \-\-check ] [ \-\-replace ] [ \-\-allow\-generic ] [ \-y ] " "file|\-"
Import devices from CSV
.RB ( name,address[,vendor[,port[,description]]] ;
blank lines, lines starting with
.BR # ,
and a header row starting
.B name,address
are skipped) or from the registry's own YAML, from a file or from stdin
.RB ( \- ).
Devices of the file are added, or update the registered device of the same name
(a CSV row leaves the hostname and the legacy-ssh setting alone; a changed address
drops the pinned host keys); the rest stay. With
.B \-\-replace
the registry becomes exactly the file, after confirmation (\c
.B \-y
skips it). Every problem is reported with its line, and nothing is written when
there is one.
.B \-\-check
validates and reports what would change without writing or taking a snapshot.
.TP
.BI "device export " "[ \-\-csv | \-\-json ]"
Print the registry: as YAML (the
.I devices.yaml
format, the default), as CSV in the import's columns with a header row, or as a
JSON array. A reduced-scope caller gets only the devices of its own scopes.
.TP
.BI "device hostkey " name " [ show | accept [ \-y ] | set SHA256:" fingerprint " ]"
The pinned ssh host keys of a device or an enrolled host, and the only way to
change them. With no action (or
.BR show )
prints each pinned key's fingerprint and its public-key line (\fBssh\-ed25519 AAAA\|...\fR, as a
.B file show
of the device's key file prints it) and the console command that shows them on
the device.
.B accept
reads the keys the device offers now, prints them beside the pinned ones, and
pins them all after confirmation (\c
.B \-y
skips it; a closed stdin cancels): run it only after comparing them with the
device console.
.BI "set SHA256:" fingerprint
reads the keys and pins only the one with that fingerprint, refusing when the
device offers none. A device that does not answer is refused and the pin stays.
Administrators only; each change is logged to syslog.
.TP
.BI "device ssh " name|address " [ \-p " port " ] [ \-\- " "ssh args" " ]"
The same as
.BR "tacctl ssh" ,
below.
.TP
.B device ssh\-config
Print an
.BR ssh_config (5)
fragment with one
.B Host
block per device and enrolled host the caller may see:
.BR HostName ,
the port when set,
.B "PubkeyAuthentication no"
and
.B "PreferredAuthentications keyboard\-interactive,password"
(WTI:
.BR password ),
the legacy algorithms of a
.B legacy\-ssh
device, and for a pinned entry
.BI "UserKnownHostsFile /var/lib/tacctl/ssh/known_hosts" ,
.BR "GlobalKnownHostsFile none" ,
.BR "StrictHostKeyChecking yes" ,
.BI "HostKeyAlias " name
and
.BR "UpdateHostKeys no" .
An unpinned entry gets a comment instead, and checks against the user's own
known_hosts. The install hint goes to stderr: save the output with
.B "tacctl device ssh\-config > ~/.ssh/tacctl.conf"
and put
.B "Include ~/.ssh/tacctl.conf"
at the top of
.I ~/.ssh/config
(an
.B Include
after a
.B Host
block belongs to that block). There is no
.B User
line: ssh logs in with your local username, which for a user of tacctl is the
tacctl name. Then a plain
.BI "ssh " name
reaches the device the way
.B "tacctl ssh"
does. tacctl only prints it; nothing is written into a home directory. Re-run it
after registry changes.
.TP
.BI "device scan " "[ \-\-full ] [ \-\-since " duration " ] [ \-\-backend " id " ]"
Read each enabled backend's log (or only
.IR id 's)
into the seen cache, from where the previous scan stopped (a journal cursor;
the auth log's inode and offset, following a rotation); the first scan reads
the last
.B stale\-days
days.
.B \-\-full
re-reads everything the logs still hold (journald's retention, twelve weeks
of rotated RADIUS logs), and
.B \-\-since
the given stretch
.RB ( 7d ,
.BR 12h ,
.BR 30m ,
.BR 2w ),
each replacing what the cache held of that backend. Then the host keys of
every entry with a pinned key are read again with
.B ssh\-keyscan
and compared with the pins (nothing is pinned). The report prints, per backend,
the stretch of log read and what it found, the host-key results, how many seen
addresses are registered, and the open notices. Records not seen for twice
.B stale\-days
are dropped. A cache that cannot be read is rebuilt.
.TP
.BI "device discover " "[ \-\-all ] [ \-\-backend " id " ]"
Scan, then list the addresses that authenticated but are not registered, with
the scope and vendor tag that answer them, first and last sighting, count,
last user, outcome, backend and NAS-Identifier, and a ready
.BI "tacctl device add " "name address"
line for each (the NAS-Identifier as the name when it is a valid, free,
non-generic one, else
.BR <name> ).
.B \-\-all
includes the addresses that were only ever refused.
.TP
.BI "device check " name " | \-\-all [ \-\-json ]"
A checklist per entry: the scope that answers the address, the vendor tag, the
last sighting (from the seen cache), whether its ssh port accepts a TCP
connection (3 seconds; a timeout may be a false alarm, as for
.BR "list \-\-probe" ),
the host keys it offers now against the pinned ones (kept for the notices;
nothing is pinned), for a registered device its SNMP name (the sysName and
.B match
or
.BR differs ,
.B no answer
or why there is none; see
.BR "config snmp" ),
and its notices.
.B \-\-json
prints the rows for scripts, the sysName as
.B sysname
(null when none was read) with
.BR sysname_match .
.SS SSH to a device \fB(tacctl ssh)\fR
.TP
.BI "ssh " name|address " [ \-p " port " ] [ \-X | \-Y ] [ \-g ] [ { \-L | \-R | \-D } " spec " ]... [ \-\- " "ssh args" " ]"
Open an ssh session to a registered device or an enrolled host, named by its
name or its registered address. tacctl resolves the name as root (the registry
is root's) and admits only an active tacctl user (in the store, not disabled,
with a password) whose scopes include the entry's, at every tier, superusers
included; an entry in no configured scope is refused to everyone, and a local
account that is not a user of tacctl is refused
.RI ( "'user' is not a tacctl user" ;
.IR "'user' has no access to scope 'scope' (device name)" ).
Each refusal is logged to syslog
.RB ( "ssh DENY user=" "\fIuser\fR " device= "\fIname\fR " scope= "\fIscope\fR " reason= "\fIreason\fR" ,
auth.warning). An admitted session is logged as
.BI "ssh user=" user " device=" name " addr=" address
(auth.info) and, when ssh ends, as
.BI "ssh end user=" user " device=" name " status=" n " duration=" seconds\fR;
.BR ssh (1)
runs as the invoking user
.RB ( "sudo \-u " "\fIuser\fR " \-H ),
with the terminal, returning its exit status. The login is always the invoking
user's own name
and the session authenticates by password only: no agent, no key, no other
login; an enrolled host's enrolment account and identity are never used. It
never runs ssh as root: run by root itself (no
.BR SUDO_USER ,
or
.BR SUDO_USER=root )
it refuses with
.IR "tacctl ssh runs ssh as the user who invoked it; run it from your own account, not as root" .
It needs a terminal on stdin
.RI ( "a terminal is required" ).
An address that is not registered is refused with the
.B "device add"
command to register it.
.IP
ssh connects to the device's hostname, else its address (an enrolled host: its
target's host), with its port unless
.B \-p
is given, and always
.B "\-o ConnectTimeout=10 \-o PubkeyAuthentication=no \-o PreferredAuthentications=keyboard\-interactive,password"
.RB ( wti :
.BR "PreferredAuthentications=password" ,
which the units need). A device with
.B legacy\-ssh
gets
\fB\-o KexAlgorithms=\:+diffie\-hellman\-group14\-sha1,\:diffie\-hellman\-group1\-sha1
\-o HostKeyAlgorithms=\:+ssh\-rsa \-o PubkeyAcceptedAlgorithms=\:+ssh\-rsa\fR
(old IOS; OpenSSH 8.5 or later). A device with pinned host keys adds
\fB\-o UserKnownHostsFile=\:/var/lib/tacctl/\:ssh/known_hosts
\-o GlobalKnownHostsFile=none \-o StrictHostKeyChecking=yes
\-o HostKeyAlias=\fR\fIname\fR\fB \-o UpdateHostKeys=no\fR,
so ssh accepts the pinned key and nothing else. An unpinned device is checked
against the user's own known_hosts, after its
.B hostkey\-unpinned
notice is printed (unless acknowledged). Arguments after
.B \-\-
follow the target, so ssh takes them as the remote command; they cannot
replace an option.
.IP
.BR \-X " and " \-Y
forward X11 (untrusted, trusted) to this server's display
.RB ( DISPLAY ,
which sudo keeps for tacctl; without one the command says to log in with
.BR "ssh \-X" );
.BR \-L ,
.B \-R
and
.B \-D
(each repeatable) are ssh's local, remote and dynamic port forwardings, with a
.I spec
of addresses, ports, host names or socket paths only. The session's log line
then carries
.BR forward= \fIkinds\fR
.RB ( x11 ", " local ", " remote ", " dynamic ).
In the login console they are for the tiers of
.B "console forwarding tiers"
only (default
.BR superuser );
any other tier is refused
.RI ( "Forwarding (\-X, \-Y, \-L, \-R, \-D) is not available to the tier tier in the console" ;
logged as
.BR "ssh DENY ... reason=forward" ).
There,
.B \-L
and
.B \-D
listen on loopback only:
.B \-g
and a bind address other than loopback
.RB ( 0.0.0.0:8443:localhost:443 ", " *:1080 ,
or an empty one) are refused
.RB ( "ssh DENY ... reason=gateway" )
unless
.B "console forwarding gateway\-ports"
is enabled.
.B \-g
lets
.B \-L
and
.B \-D
ports listen on every address, as ssh's
.BR \-g .
.IP
From a session of the login console (the console marks each line with
.BR TACCTL_CONSOLE= \fIsession\fR),
ssh reads no configuration file and opens nothing but the session: it starts
with
.B "\-F /dev/null \-o PermitLocalCommand=no \-o ControlMaster=no \-o ClearAllForwardings=yes \-o ForwardAgent=no"
.RB ( ClearAllForwardings
left out when the session forwards) and
.B "\-o EscapeChar=none"
(unless
.BR "console ssh\-escape enable" ),
the target follows
.B \-\-
so no word after it is an option, and words after the name's
.B \-\-
that start with
.B \-
are refused. An entry with no pinned host key is refused
.RI ( "'name' has no pinned host key, so the console does not connect to it; an administrator pins it: tacctl device hostkey name accept" ;
logged with
.BR reason=unpinned ).
Its log lines end with
.BI console= session\fR.
.IP
When ssh ends with status 255 on a pinned device, tacctl reads the device's
keys again; if the pinned key is not among them it prints the pinned and the
offered fingerprints, the console command that shows the key on the device,
and
.BI "tacctl device hostkey " name " accept"
or
.BI "set SHA256:" fingerprint
to re-pin after verifying. The read-only tier may run it (the tiers sudoers
rules grant
.B "tacctl ssh *"
and
.BR "tacctl device ssh *" ).
.SS Login console \fB(tacctl console)\fR
The login console
.RB ( tacctl\-console )
is the login shell of tacctl users on the tacctl server: tacctl commands and
ssh to registered devices, nothing else. It is on for every tier by default
(the defaults apply while
.I /etc/tacctl/console.yaml
is absent); local accounts that are not tacctl users are never touched. These
commands change
.I console.yaml
only, after a snapshot (mode 0600; backup diff and restore include it), and
never an account or sshd: the accounts' login shells and sshd's drop-in follow
it when the provisioning of this server applies it
.RB ( "host sync" " of the host enrolled with " \-\-local ).
Every change to the tier switches, a user override, agent forwarding or the
forwarding tiers prints
.BI "Apply to the accounts: tacctl host sync " name\fR.
The idle timeout, the ssh escape and the system shell are read by each console
session when it starts.
.TP
.B console
With no subcommand (or with
.BR help ),
prints a usage summary and exits 0.
.TP
.B console show
The tiers' switches, the settings, and for each user of the scope of the host
enrolled with
.B \-\-local
the tier, the effective shell
.RB ( console
or
.BR bash )
and why
.RI ( "user override" ", " "tier readonly" ", " "tier readonly disabled" ).
Then the state of the server's pieces: the
.I /usr/local/bin/tacctl\-console
symlink, the
.I /etc/shells
line, sshd's drop-in, and what
.B "sshd \-T \-C user="\fIuser\fR
reports for a console user
.RB ( allowtcpforwarding ,
.BR allowagentforwarding ,
.BR forcecommand ,
.BR pubkeyauthentication ).
A red warning says so when the drop-in is missing, sshd does not force the
console, still allows forwarding, or allows key logins, because a console
user could then forward ports past the device registry, run programs or sftp,
or log in without TACACS+. A server not enrolled with
.B \-\-local
says so and lists no users. Operator tier and up.
.TP
.BI "console tiers " "[ " tier " " "{ enable | disable }" " ]"
Show the switches, or one, or set the console for a tier
.RB ( readonly ,
.BR operator ,
.BR superuser ).
.TP
.BI "console user " "name " "[ { enable | disable | clear } ]"
Show or set one user's override, which wins over the tier's switch;
.B clear
returns the user to the tier rule. The user must exist in the store to be
given an override.
.TP
.BI "console idle\-timeout " "[ " min " ]"
Minutes idle at the prompt before the session ends, 0 to 1440 (0: never;
default 30).
.TP
.BI "console agent\-forwarding " "[ { enable | disable } ]"
Opt in to ssh agent forwarding for console users (default disabled).
.TP
.BI "console forwarding tiers " "[ " csv "|none ]"
The tiers whose console logins may forward X11 and TCP ports (default
.BR superuser ):
sshd's drop-in gives the members of
.B tac\-console
and of the tier's group
.RB ( tac\-superuser ", " tac\-operator ", " tac\-readonly )
.B "X11Forwarding yes"
and
.B "AllowTcpForwarding yes"
in a block of their own before the console's
.RB ( "ssh \-X" ,
.BR \-L ,
.BR \-R ,
.B \-D
and
.B \-J
through this server), and the console's
.B ssh
to a device accepts
.BR \-X ,
.BR \-Y ,
.BR \-L ,
.B \-R
and
.BR \-D .
Agent, stream-local and tunnel forwarding stay as for every console user.
.TP
.BI "console forwarding gateway\-ports " "[ { enable | disable } ]"
Opt in to forwarded ports that listen on other addresses than loopback, for
the tiers of
.B "console forwarding tiers"
(default disabled). Enabled, their blocks in sshd's drop-in set
.BR "GatewayPorts clientspecified" :
an
.B "ssh \-R"
to this server listens on the address the client names
.RB ( "\-R 0.0.0.0:8080:host:80"
on every address; none named: loopback), and the console's
.B ssh
takes
.B \-g
and a bind address on
.B \-L
and
.BR \-D .
Anyone who reaches this server can then connect to those ports. Every other
console login keeps
.BR "GatewayPorts no" ,
and
.B "console check"
warns when sshd applies anything else to it.
.TP
.BI "console ssh\-escape " "[ { enable | disable } ]"
Opt in to ssh's escape character (\fB~.\fR, \fB~C\fR) in the console's ssh
sessions (default disabled).
.TP
.BI "console system\-shell tiers " "[ " csv "|none ]"
The tiers that may start their system shell from the console (default
.BR superuser ).
.TP
.BI "console system\-shell path " "[ " path " ]"
The system shell, default
.IR /bin/bash .
It must be an absolute path to an executable file listed in
.IR /etc/shells .
.TP
.B console install
Puts the server's pieces in place, as
.B host sync
of the host enrolled with
.B \-\-local
does: the line
.I /usr/local/bin/tacctl\-console
in
.IR /etc/shells ,
and sshd's drop-in
.I /etc/ssh/sshd_config.d/tacctl\-console.conf
for the members of
.BR tac\-console :
.B "ForceCommand /usr/local/bin/tacctl\-console"
(every login, remote command and subsystem, sftp included, reaches the
console, which reads the client's command from
.BR SSH_ORIGINAL_COMMAND ),
.B "DisableForwarding yes"
(left out, with
.BR "AllowAgentForwarding yes" ,
when
.B console agent\-forwarding
is enabled; for the tiers of
.B "console forwarding tiers"
a block before it sets
.BR "DisableForwarding no" ,
.B "AllowTcpForwarding yes"
and
.BR "X11Forwarding yes" ),
.BR "AllowTcpForwarding no" ,
.BR "AllowStreamLocalForwarding no" ,
.BR "X11Forwarding no" ,
.BR "PermitTunnel no" ,
.BR "PermitTTY yes" ,
.B "PubkeyAuthentication no"
(a key login would bypass TACACS+),
.B ClientAliveInterval 300
and
.BR "ClientAliveCountMax 2" .
Each change of the drop-in is checked with
.BR "sshd \-t" ;
when sshd refuses it the previous file comes back and the command exits 1,
otherwise sshd is reloaded
.RB ( "systemctl reload ssh.service" ,
or
.BR sshd.service ).
Each piece is reported
.BR Installed: ,
.B Updated:
or
.BR Unchanged: ,
then the check below runs. Refused when
.I /usr/local/bin/tacctl\-console
is missing
.RB ( "tacctl upgrade" " makes it)."
.TP
.B console remove
Takes the drop-in (checked with
.BR "sshd \-t" ,
sshd reloaded) and the
.I /etc/shells
line away. Refused, nothing changed, while an account still has the console
as its login shell; they are named, with the way to give them
.I /bin/bash
back.
.TP
.B console check
Whether sshd applies the console's settings: the drop-in present and this
release's, the
.B Include
of
.I sshd_config.d/*.conf
in
.IR /etc/ssh/sshd_config ,
and
.B "sshd \-T"
for the first account whose shell is the console. Exit 1, with the red
warning, when anything is off. Operator tier and up.
.PP
Only the operator tier and up may run
.B "console show"
and
.BR "console check" ;
everything else needs an administrator.
.PP
The console asks tacctl for its settings with the hidden
.BR _console\-policy ,
which the tiers sudoers rules grant to every tier.
.SS Console sessions \fB(tacctl\-console)\fR
.I /usr/local/bin/tacctl\-console
is a symbolic link to the tacctl binary; started under that name (as a login
shell,
.BR \-tacctl\-console )
tacctl is the console: the interactive shell
.RB ( "tacctl shell" )
with the prompt
.IB host "> "
(the server's short host name) and the banner
.IR "tacctl\~console on host \(em type 'help'. Devices: device list. This session is logged." ,
whose lines run as
.PP
.RS
.B sudo
.RB [ \-n ]
.BI TACCTL_CONSOLE= session
.B /usr/local/bin/tacctl
.I words
.RE
.PP
with the rule of
.B tacctl shell
for
.BR \-n .
It starts nothing else. The login environment is discarded except
.BR TERM ,
.BR LANG ,
.BR LC_* ,
.BR HOME ,
.BR USER ,
.BR LOGNAME ,
.BR SSH_CONNECTION ,
.B SSH_CLIENT
and
.BR SSH_TTY ;
.B PATH
is
.I /usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin
and
.B SHELL
the console. At the start it asks the root side for its settings
.RB ( "sudo \-n tacctl _console\-policy" );
without an answer the defaults apply (30 minutes idle, no system shell). The
session ends after the idle timeout at the prompt
.RB ( "console idle\-timeout" ;
not while a line runs).
.PP
.BI "\-c " line
(the remote command sshd passes:
.BR "ssh " "\fIserver\fR " "'user list'" )
runs one tacctl line, with the connection as its standard input. A string
that is not one line beginning with a tacctl command or
.B help
\(em
.BR scp ,
.BR sftp ,
.BR rsync ,
any program, the shell's own words, an empty string \(em is refused with
.I "the tacctl\~console does not run programs; file transfer is not available"
and exit status 126, and nothing runs. Any other argument is refused the same
way
.RI ( "the tacctl\~console takes no options" ).
With standard input not a terminal and no
.BR \-c ,
the lines of the input run as a batch, as in
.BR "tacctl shell" .
.PP
.B system\-shell
(the console's own word; interactive sessions only) starts the user's system
shell
.RB ( "console system\-shell path" ,
default
.IR /bin/bash )
as the user, without arguments, with the console's environment and
.BR SHELL= \fIpath\fR;
the console's idle timer does not run meanwhile, and sshd's drop-in still
governs the connection. It is open to the tiers
.B "console system\-shell tiers"
names (default: superusers only); otherwise it is refused with
.IR "system-shell is not available for the tier tier on this console. An administrator enables it with: tacctl console system-shell tiers ..."
(status 1). In a batch or through
.B \-c
it is refused with status 126.
.PP
Syslog (tag
.BR tacctl\-console ):
.BI "console start session=" id " user=" user " from=" address " tty=" tty " mode=" interactive|command|batch\fR,
.BI "console end session=" id " user=" user " reason=" exit|eof|idle|hangup|signal|command|failed " lines=" n " status=" n\fR,
.BI "console DENY session=" id " user=" user " reason=command first=" word
(auth.warning),
.BI "console system\-shell start" \fR,
.B end
.RB ( status= ", " duration= )
and
.BR DENY .
Each line is also in sudo's log, and
.B tacctl ssh
logs
.BI console= id\fR.
.SH RADIUS DEVICE CONFIGURATIONS
.BR "config cisco" ,
.B "config juniper"
and
.B "config wti"
render the configuration for logging in to a device against the RADIUS
backend when given
.BR "--protocol radius" ,
or without the flag for a scope that resolves to RADIUS (its auth-method,
else its only protocol;
.BR "scope auth-method" ).
The server address, the authentication and accounting ports (the
.B auth
and
.B acct
listeners shown by
.BR "config listen --backend radius show" )
and the scope's shared secret are filled in; the management ACL, the
exec timeout, the scope's
.B aaa-order
(the server first, or local first) and the local fallback behave as in
the TACACS+ output. Without the flag, and for a scope that does not
resolve to RADIUS, the output is the TACACS+ configuration, unchanged.
.PP
Cisco output is a
.B "radius server"
block,
.B "aaa group server radius"
(named by
.BR "scope radius-group" ),
.BR "aaa authentication login" ,
.BR "aaa authorization exec"
(the privilege level comes from the reply's
.BR "Cisco-AVPair shell:priv-lvl=N" ,
which the scope must send: see
.BR "scope vendor-attrs" )
and
.BR "aaa accounting exec" ,
with the same
.B "privilege exec level"
mappings. Juniper output is
.BR "system radius-server" ,
.BR authentication-order ,
.B "system accounting destination radius"
and the template users that the reply's
.B Juniper-Local-User-Name
selects, with the per-class allow-commands and deny-commands rules,
which stay in force because the class is local to the device.
.PP
RADIUS has no per-command authorization and no command accounting, and
tacctl's RADIUS accepts PAP only. Each RADIUS output says under the
configuration what the Access-Accept carries for the device
.RB ( Service-Type
and the vendor's attribute, enabled for the scope or for tagged
addresses only), and which TACACS+ features are not available
(rules set with
.B "group commands"
are not enforced by the server).
.PP
tacctl refuses, with an error and no output, when the configuration
would look right and not work: the RADIUS backend is not enabled
.RB ( "backend enable radius" ),
the scope's
.B "scope protocols"
filter leaves out
.BR radius ,
the scope sends the vendor's attribute to none of its devices (neither
enabled with
.B "scope vendor-attrs"
nor an address tagged with
.BR "scope devices" ;
the error prints the command that enables it),
or the scope secret holds a character that IOS or Junos read as syntax
(whitespace, quotes, a backtick, a non-ASCII character, or any of
.BR "? ! # $ \\e ; { } [ ] | & < > , * ( )" ).
Letters, digits and
.B ". _ + / = : @ % ^ ~ -"
paste unquoted, and
.B "scope secret generate"
makes such a secret. The secret is shared with TACACS+, so changing it
means changing it on every device of the scope. A secret longer than 63
characters renders with a warning. A listener bound to one IPv4 address
supplies the server address; an IPv6 or loopback bind is warned about.
Operator overrides
.IR cisco-radius.template ,
.I juniper-radius.template
and
.I wti-radius.template
in the templates directory work as for the TACACS+ templates; see
.BR FILES .
.SH CONVENTIONS
The following patterns apply uniformly across subcommand families.
.TP
.B No arguments
Dispatcher commands
.RB ( user ,
.BR group ,
.BR config ,
.BR log ,
.BR backup ,
.BR hash ,
and every nested dispatcher such as
.BR "scope prefixes" ,
.BR "scope secret" ,
.BR "scope aaa-order" ,
.BR "scope exec-timeout" ,
.BR "scope tacacs-group" ,
.BR "scope radius-group" ,
.BR "scope auth-method" ,
.BR "scope vendor-attrs" ,
.BR "scope devices" ,
.BR "scope mgmt-acl" ,
.BR "user scope" ,
or
.BR "group privilege" )
print their own usage and return without side effects.
Scalar getter/setters
.RB ( "config loglevel" ,
.BR "config listen" ,
.BR "config metrics" ,
.BR "config password-age" ,
.BR "config bcrypt-cost" ,
.BR "config password-min-length" ,
.BR "config secret-min-length" ,
.BR "config branch" ,
.BR "scope default" )
print the current value when called with no arguments.
.TP
.B Multi-item input
Every
.B add
/
.B remove
subcommand that takes a CIDR or a Cisco exec command accepts either a
single value or a comma-separated list
.RI ( "cidr1,cidr2,..." ).
Every input is validated first; a bad entry aborts the entire
operation without writing anything.
.TP
.B CIDR semantics
Every CIDR-list subcommand
.RB ( "scope prefixes" ,
.BR "config allow" ,
.BR "config deny" ,
.BR "config mgmt-acl" )
canonicalizes input before storage:
.B 10.1.5.5/24
becomes
.BR 10.1.5.0/24 ;
.B 2001:DB8::/32
becomes
.BR 2001:db8::/32 .
Exact duplicates (after canonicalization) are rejected as no-ops on
.BR add .
Overlapping CIDRs of different prefix lengths coexist
.RI ( "10.0.0.0/8 and 10.99.0.0/16 can both be present" ).
Stored order is by broadcast-address ascending (IPv4 before IPv6):
disjoint ranges sort by their end address, and an overlapping subnet
falls immediately above its containing supernet (the subnet's range
ends before the supernet's). This groups related CIDRs together and
gives tacquito's provider selector the "most-specific first among
overlaps" ordering it needs so that a narrower scope wins a first-
match lookup over a broader scope that contains it.
.TP
.B Scope prefix invariants
Every CIDR (after canonicalization) belongs to
.B exactly one
scope. Adding a prefix already owned by a different scope is rejected
with a message naming the owning scope; the operator must remove it
from the owning scope first with
.B "scope prefixes " \fIowner\fR " remove " \fIcidr\fR
before re-adding it elsewhere. Overlapping prefixes
.I across
scopes are allowed and routed correctly — a scope with
.B 10.5.0.0/16
coexists with a scope holding the supernet
.BR 10.0.0.0/8 .
.PP
To make first-match-wins honor cross-scope specificity, tacctl emits
one
.B secrets:
entry per (scope, prefix) pair — a scope with N prefixes becomes N
entries that share
.BR name: " and " secret.key: .
Entries are re-sorted globally by prefix specificity
(v4 before v6; smaller broadcast first) after every mutation, so
tacquito's slice-ordered provider walk routes each client IP to the
narrowest scope that contains it. The CLI continues to present the
logical one-scope-per-bundle view via
.BR "scope list" ", " "scope show" ", and " "config show" .
.PP
Do not hand-edit the
.B secrets:
block under multi-prefix scopes — the same-name entries must carry
identical
.B secret.key
values.
.B config validate
reports any divergence.
.TP
.B "clear and remove \-\-all"
.B clear
and
.B "remove \-\-all"
subcommands always prompt with
.B [y/N]
and print a warning about the resulting posture (for example, "no
clients can connect" or "fails open").
.TP
.B "Filters and opt-ins"
A setting that narrows something down is a filter: its verbs are
.B set
and
.BR clear ,
and empty (cleared) means everything
.RB ( "scope protocols" ,
.BR "config allow" ,
.BR "config deny" ).
A setting that turns something on is an opt-in: its verbs are
.B enable
and
.BR disable ,
and nothing is on until it is enabled
.RB ( "backend enable|disable" ,
.BR "scope vendor-attrs" ).
An opt-in has no
.BR set ,
.B clear
or
.BR none ,
so that nothing in it can read as "empty means all".
.TP
.B "Membership lists"
A list that names exactly what is in is a membership list, and empty
means nothing, not everything
.RB ( "user scope" ,
the scopes a user can authenticate from: none means nowhere;
.BR "scope prefixes" ,
the clients a scope serves: emptying it removes the scope).
Its verbs are
.B add
and
.BR remove :
.B "remove \-\-all"
empties it, and
.B "user scope"
replaces its whole list with
.BR replace .
.TP
.B Service restart
Changes to the store, and to the
.I tacctl.yaml
settings a backend renders (command rules, listeners,
.BR backends.* ),
re-render every enabled backend and restart the backends whose files
changed. Settings only device configurations read (mgmt-acl permits and
names, per-group priv-exec mappings, per-scope AAA order, exec timeout and
group labels) and tacctl's own tunables (bcrypt cost, password age, \&...)
restart nothing.
.TP
.B Prompts
Every confirmation prompt cancels, with a message, when standard input is
closed.
.B \-y
(or
.BR \-\-yes ,
.B \-\-force
where documented) answers yes.
.TP
.B Flags
Long-form flags
.RI ( --hash ,
.IR --scopes ,
.IR --scope ,
.IR --prefixes ,
.IR --secret ,
.IR --protocols ,
.IR --vendor-attrs ,
.IR --match ,
.IR --action ,
.IR --branch ,
.IR --protocol ,
.IR --method ,
.IR --backend ,
.IR --listener ,
.IR --server ,
.IR --name ,
.IR --port ,
.IR --identity ,
.IR --output )
take a single argument;
.IR --default ,
.IR --force ,
.IR --legacy ,
.IR --check ,
.IR --replace ,
.IR --json ,
.IR --local ,
.IR --all ,
.IR --build-on-host ,
.IR --allow-uid-mismatch ,
.I --remove-home
and
.I -y
take none. Required positional arguments come before flags.
.SH BACKENDS
.TP
.B tacacs
TACACS+, served by tacquito built from source; installed by
.B "tacctl install"
and enabled by default.
.TP
.B radius
RADIUS, served by FreeRADIUS from the distribution's package; installed and
enabled by
.BR "tacctl backend enable radius" .
.PP
Every change renders the configuration of every enabled backend from the
store, all or none, and restarts only the backends whose files changed.
With more than one backend enabled,
.BR status ,
.BR "config show" ,
.B "config validate"
and
.B log
print a labelled section per backend
.RB ( "== Backend: radius (radius, freeradius) ==" ).
.PP
.B Listeners
are
.BI listeners. backend . name
in
.I tacctl.yaml
.RB ( "config listen" ).
The TACACS+ listener
.B default
(tcp :49) runs in
.BR tacquito.service ;
every further one is an instance
.BI tacquito@ name .service
of
.BR tacquito@.service ,
.B PartOf
and
.B WantedBy
.BR tacquito.service ,
so
.B "systemctl restart tacquito"
covers them all. Each unit has a rendered drop-in
.IR <unit>.d/tacctl.conf .
RADIUS listeners
.RB ( auth ,
udp :1812;
.BR acct ,
udp :1813) are
.B listen
sections of tacctl's FreeRADIUS configuration. TACACS+ over TLS is reserved
in the schema and not implemented.
.PP
.B RADIUS.
.B "backend enable radius"
installs
.B freeradius
and
.B freeradius\-utils
(Debian/Ubuntu; RHEL family from AppStream) and starts the distribution's
unit
.RB ( freeradius
or
.BR radiusd )
with a drop-in that runs tacctl's own instance
.RB ( "\-n tacctl\-radius \-D" " <its dictionary>" ):
the package's configuration is not read, edited or unlinked, so there is no
EAP, inner tunnel, proxying or default client. A unit that is already
running or enabled without tacctl's drop-in is somebody's RADIUS server and
is refused. One client per prefix of every scope RADIUS serves, with the
scope's secret; a user authenticates only from a client in one of their
scopes; disabled users and the
.B root
sink never do. PAP only, against the stored bcrypt hashes (no CHAP,
MS-CHAP or EAP). No per-command authorization and no command accounting.
An Access-Accept carries
.B Service-Type
always, and a vendor's privilege attribute only where the scope opts in
.RB ( "scope vendor-attrs" ", " "scope devices" ).
FreeRADIUS 3.2.5 and 3.0.27 log a "BlastRADIUS check" block for a client the
first time it sends Message-Authenticator and require it from that client
from then on. Verified against FreeRADIUS 3.0.20, 3.0.27 and 3.2.5;
nothing was tested on Cisco, Juniper or WTI hardware.
.SH STORE AND GENERATED CONFIGURATION
.I /etc/tacctl/store.yaml
(users, groups, scopes, filters) and
.I /etc/tacctl/tacctl.yaml
(settings) are canonical. Generated from them:
.I /etc/tacquito/tacquito.yaml
and the tacquito unit drop-ins; for RADIUS
.IR tacctl\-radius.conf ,
.I tacctl\-radius.users
and
.I tacctl\-radius\-dictionary/dictionary
in the FreeRADIUS configuration directory.
.I /etc/tacctl/rendered.json
holds their checksums.
.PP
A change is checked against hand edits, snapshotted, written, rendered into
a staging area for every enabled backend and proved there (read back, the
daemon's own config check), then installed, and the backends whose files
changed are restarted; a failure at any step puts the store,
.I tacctl.yaml
and every generated file back.
.PP
.B Drift.
A generated file that no longer matches its checksum was edited by hand:
.B status
and
.B "config validate"
show a
.B DRIFT
line and every change is refused (exit 3). Keep a hand edit of
.I tacquito.yaml
with
.B "store import \-\-replace"
then
.BR "config render \-\-force" ;
discard it with
.B "config render \-\-force"
(a copy goes to
.IR /etc/tacctl/backups/legacy/ ).
RADIUS files can only be re-rendered.
.PP
.B Legacy read-only mode.
Without a store (an upgrade whose gate stopped, or after
.BR "store rollback" )
tacctl reads
.I tacquito.yaml
directly: read commands work, commands that change users, groups, scopes
or filters are refused with
.IR "store not initialised" .
Only TACACS+ exists in that mode.
.SH UPGRADING
.B "tacctl upgrade"
pulls the clone in
.I /opt/tacctl
and, when the installed binary was not built from the commit now checked out
(also after a pull that changed only documentation), builds it again and
re-executes it once; the version printed by
.B "tacctl version"
therefore always matches the clone. Then, in this order, it:
.IP 1. 4
moves
.IR tacctl.yaml ,
.IR linux\-hosts ,
.IR linux\-uids ,
.I backups/
and
.I templates/
from
.I /etc/tacquito
to
.IR /etc/tacctl ,
leaving links at the old paths (idempotent; a regular file written there by
other software wins over the moved copy, which is kept under
.IR backups/legacy/ );
.IP 2. 4
installs
.B tacquito.service
and
.BR tacquito@.service ,
imports a hand-managed drop-in
.I tacctl\-overrides.conf
into
.I tacctl.yaml
once, renders
.I tacctl.conf
in its place and keeps the old file under
.IR backups/legacy/ ;
.IP 3. 4
on an install that has no store yet, runs the store gate:
.B "store import \-\-check"
(import, render, equivalence of what tacquito loads from both files, a load
test of the rendered file with the new binary on a loopback port). Only when
it passes is the store written, the old file kept as
.IR backups/legacy/tacquito.yaml.pre\-store. <timestamp>,
.I tacquito.yaml
rendered and tacquito restarted.
.PP
When the gate stops (unrepresentable content, a render that is not
equivalent, the tacquito binary missing) the upgrade still completes, leaves
.I tacquito.yaml
and the daemon as they were, and tacctl is in legacy read-only mode. It never
forces either through. Fix what
.B "store import \-\-check"
reports and upgrade again, or accept the difference:
.B "store import"
.RB [ \-\-force ],
then
.BR "config render \-\-force" .
.B "store rollback"
undoes the move into the store.
.SH FILES
.SS tacctl
.TP
.I /etc/tacctl/
State directory, mode 0700, root only.
.TP
.I /etc/tacctl/store.yaml
The canonical users, groups, scopes and connection filters (shared secrets and
password hashes included). Mode 0600, root only. Every command that changes
them renders each enabled backend's config from it. Print it with
.BR "tacctl store show" .
.TP
.I /etc/tacctl/tacctl.yaml
Operator overrides for tacctl tunables and settings (bcrypt cost, password /
secret minimum lengths, password age warning, default scope, default host
method, mgmt-acl permits + names, per-group command rules and Cisco
priv-exec mappings, per-scope device-config settings, listeners,
.BR backends.* ).
The canonical defaults are built into tacctl and printed by
.BR "tacctl config defaults" .
Only set keys you want to change \(em missing keys inherit from the
defaults, and setting a key back to the default value removes it. An
empty overrides file is deleted. Every write is schema-validated
(out-of-range numbers, bad ACL names, malformed CIDRs, colliding listeners,
typo'd keys are rejected before anything is written). A file that does
not parse is never written into: a setter refuses with
.RI "\(lqtacctl.yaml: could not parse " path ": " reason \(rq
and exits 1, leaving it as it is, and commands that only read use the
defaults and warn once on stderr; fix or remove the file. Inspect the merged
view with
.BR "tacctl config dump" ;
re-check the file with
.BR "tacctl config validate" .
.TP
.I /etc/tacctl/devices.yaml
The device registry (see
.BR "DEVICE REGISTRY" ):
registered device names with address, vendor, port, hostname,
description, legacy-ssh, pinned host keys and acknowledged notices, and the
registry's settings
.RB ( stale_days ,
.BR generic_names ).
Mode 0600, root only; absent means no devices. Edit it with
.BR "tacctl device" ,
which locks it, validates the result and replaces it atomically. Snapshots
include it. The pinned keys of enrolled hosts are kept under
.BR hosts: .
.TP
.I /etc/tacctl/snmp.yaml
The SNMP community, or the v3 user and its passphrases, that
.B "device add"
and
.B "device check"
read a device's sysName with. Mode 0600, root only, written atomically by
.BR "tacctl config snmp" ;
never printed. Absent means none.
.TP
.I /etc/tacctl/console.yaml
The login console's switches per tier, per-user overrides and settings
.RB ( idle_timeout ,
.BR agent_forwarding ,
.BR ssh_escape ,
.BR system_shell ,
.BR system_shell_tiers ,
.BR forwarding_tiers ,
.BR list_max ,
the number of completions the shell lists without asking).
Mode 0600, root only; absent means the defaults (the console on for every
tier). Edit it with
.BR "tacctl console" ,
which locks it, validates the result and replaces it atomically. Snapshots
include it.
.TP
.I /usr/local/bin/tacctl\-console
The login console: a symbolic link to
.IR /usr/local/bin/tacctl ,
made by
.B install
and refreshed by
.BR upgrade .
.TP
.I /etc/ssh/sshd_config.d/tacctl\-console.conf
sshd's drop-in for the members of
.B tac\-console
(the accounts whose shell is the console): the console forced, every
forwarding closed, key logins off. Written by
.B host sync
of the host enrolled with
.B \-\-local
and
.BR "console install" ,
checked with
.BR "sshd \-t" ,
refreshed by
.BR upgrade ,
removed by
.BR "console remove" ,
.B host unenroll
of that host and
.BR uninstall .
Do not edit.
.TP
.I /etc/shells
Gets the line
.I /usr/local/bin/tacctl\-console
with the drop-in, and loses it with it.
.TP
.I /var/lib/tacctl/ssh/known_hosts
Generated from
.I devices.yaml
on every registry write (and after
.BR "backup restore" ):
one
.I "name type key"
line per pinned host key, for ssh's
.B HostKeyAlias
lookup. Mode 0644, root-owned, in a directory 0755 under
.I /var/lib/tacctl
(0711: users pass through it, only root lists it), so every user's ssh can
read it;
.I /etc/tacctl
is root's alone. Do not edit it.
.TP
.I /etc/tacctl/rendered.json
The checksum of every file tacctl rendered, from which
.B status
and
.B config validate
report a hand edit as drift.
.TP
.I /etc/tacctl/backups/
Automatic snapshots of the canonical files, one directory per snapshot, last
30 retained, root-only;
.I legacy/
holds old-style
.I tacquito.yaml.<timestamp>
backups, the pre-store config and files a render or
the state migration displaced.
.TP
.I /etc/tacctl/templates/
Host-specific overrides for the device config templates
.RI ( cisco ,
.IR cisco-legacy ,
.IR juniper ,
.IR wti ,
and for
.BR "--protocol radius"
.IR cisco-radius ,
.IR juniper-radius " and " wti-radius ).
A file named
.I <name>.template
here is used in place of the one shipped in
.IR config/templates/ .
Install puts a copy of each shipped template here, and
.B "tacctl upgrade"
replaces one with the new release's only while it is as tacctl wrote it:
.I .shipped.sha256
records what tacctl wrote (in
.BR sha256sum (1)
format). A template that was changed is kept, the new release's version is
written beside it as
.IR <name>.template.new ,
which is never used for rendering, and the upgrade warns, naming both;
compare them with
.BR diff (1),
or take the new version with
.BR mv (1).
A template that has no record there counts
as unchanged when it is byte for byte a version tacctl shipped, according to
the git history of
.IR /opt/tacctl .
.TP
.IR /etc/tacctl/linux\-hosts ", " /etc/tacctl/linux\-uids
The registry of enrolled Linux hosts (with each host's method) and the
UID each user gets on every host.
.TP
.I /var/lib/tacctl/linux/
The pam_tacplus source tarball and the modules built in containers.
.TP
.I /var/lib/tacctl/devices\-seen.json
The seen cache of
.BR "device scan" :
what the logs showed of each address, where each backend's next scan starts,
and the last host-key re-scan of each entry. Mode 0600, root only; derived
from the logs, not snapshotted, rebuilt by
.BR "device scan \-\-full" .
.TP
.IR /etc/sudoers.d/tacctl ", " /etc/sudoers.d/tacctl\-tiers
Optional sudoers rules. Installed only via
.B "tacctl config sudoers install"
and
.BR "tacctl config sudoers tiers install" .
.TP
.I /usr/local/bin/tacctl
The tacctl binary, built on the host from the clone in
.I /opt/tacctl
by
.B "tacctl install"
and
.BR "tacctl upgrade" .
.TP
.I /opt/tacctl/
Clone of the management (tacctl) repository: the source tacctl is built from
(Go sources and vendored dependencies), the shipped templates, units,
patches and this manual, and the repository
.B "tacctl upgrade"
pulls.
.I /opt/tacctl/bin/tacctl.sh
is the bootstrap: it installs the Go toolchain if needed, builds the binary
when it is missing or out of date, and runs it.
.TP
.I /etc/bash_completion.d/tacctl
The bash completion script, generated by
.BR "tacctl completion bash" .
.TP
.I /usr/share/man/man1/tacctl.1.gz
This manual.
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/tacctl/history
The
.B tacctl shell
history of the invoking user: mode 0600, the newest 1000 lines, redacted.
.SS TACACS+ (tacquito)
.TP
.I /etc/tacquito/tacquito.yaml
TACACS+ server configuration, rendered by tacctl from the store. Mode 0640,
owned by
.BR tacquito : tacquito .
.I /etc/tacquito
also holds a copy of the README, and the old paths of
.IR tacctl.yaml ,
.IR linux\-hosts ,
.IR linux\-uids ,
.I backups
and
.I templates
lead into
.IR /etc/tacctl .
.TP
.I /etc/systemd/system/tacquito.service
Systemd unit file (the default listener).
.TP
.I /etc/systemd/system/tacquito@.service
Template unit, one instance per further listener.
.TP
.I /etc/systemd/system/tacquito.service.d/tacctl.conf
Systemd drop-in holding the listen address, log level, metrics address
and accounting log of the unit. Rendered by tacctl from
.I tacctl.yaml
.RB ( "tacctl config listen" ,
.BR "tacctl config loglevel" ,
.BR "tacctl config metrics" );
edits are overwritten. Instances have
.IR tacquito@<name>.service.d/tacctl.conf .
.TP
.I /var/log/tacquito/accounting.log
Per-session accounting records
.RI ( accounting\-<name>.log
per further listener). Rotated daily, retained 90 days
.RI ( /etc/logrotate.d/tacquito ).
.TP
.I /usr/local/bin/tacquito
The tacquito server binary.
.TP
.I /usr/local/bin/tacquito-hashgen
Standalone bcrypt hash generator shipped with tacquito.
.TP
.I /opt/tacquito-src/
Clone of the tacquito server source code, used to rebuild the binary
during
.BR "tacctl upgrade" .
.SS RADIUS (FreeRADIUS)
.I <raddb>
is
.I /etc/freeradius/3.0
(Debian/Ubuntu) or
.I /etc/raddb
(RHEL family);
.I <logs>
is
.I /var/log/freeradius
or
.IR /var/log/radius .
No file of the FreeRADIUS package is edited.
.TP
.IR <raddb>/tacctl\-radius.conf ", " <raddb>/tacctl\-radius.users
tacctl's FreeRADIUS configuration (listeners, clients, policy) and its users
file (bcrypt hashes), rendered from the store; mode 0640, root and the
daemon's group.
.TP
.I <raddb>/tacctl\-radius\-dictionary/dictionary
The dictionary the daemon is started with: the package's, then the WTI
vendor and tacctl's internal attributes.
.TP
.IR /etc/systemd/system/freeradius.service.d/tacctl.conf " (" radiusd.service.d " on RHEL)"
Drop-in that starts the distribution's unit as tacctl's instance; present
exactly while the backend is enabled.
.TP
.IR <logs>/tacctl\-auth.log ", " <logs>/tacctl\-accounting.log ", " <logs>/tacctl\-radius.log
One line per Access-Accept or Access-Reject; accounting detail records; the
daemon's log
.RI ( /etc/logrotate.d/tacctl\-radius ).
.SS Linux hosts
.TP
.IR /etc/pam.d/tacctl\-* ", " /etc/tacctl\-pam_radius.conf ", " /var/lib/tacctl\-client/
On an enrolled host: the PAM files tacctl writes (root-only; for tacplus
they hold the shared secret), the pam_radius_auth server file (0600), and
the client's state.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Install on a fresh host:
.B tacctl install
.TP
Add a superuser:
.B tacctl user add alice superuser
.TP
Restrict device subnets in the default scope:
.B tacctl scope prefixes lab add 10.1.0.0/24,172.16.5.0/24
.TP
Build a multi-environment scope hierarchy (broader prod scopes with
narrower lab scopes carved out — the /24 wins first-match for 10.10.99.x
clients, the /16 catches the rest of 10.10.x):
.nf
.B "  tacctl scope add prod_dc_east --prefixes 10.10.0.0/16 --secret generate"
.B "  tacctl scope add lab_east     --prefixes 10.10.99.0/24 --secret generate"
.fi
.TP
Trace which scope a client IP falls into:
.B tacctl scope lookup 10.10.99.7
.TP
Grant a user full production access across two datacenters:
.B tacctl user scope alice add prod_dc_east,prod_dc_west
.TP
Generate a Cisco-ready configuration for the prod scope:
.B tacctl config cisco --scope prod_dc_east
.TP
Generate legacy IOS 12.x syntax for an older device (e.g. an ITP on 12.4):
.B tacctl config cisco --scope prod_dc_east --legacy
.TP
Print the serial-menu walkthrough for a WTI console server in the lab scope:
.B tacctl config wti --scope lab_east
.TP
Serve the same users over RADIUS, with Cisco and Juniper privilege attributes for prod:
.nf
.B "  tacctl backend enable radius"
.B "  tacctl scope vendor-attrs prod_dc_east enable cisco,juniper"
.B "  tacctl config cisco --scope prod_dc_east --protocol radius"
.fi
.TP
Tag one address of a scope as a WTI unit (it gets WTI-Super and nothing else):
.B tacctl scope devices lab_east set 10.10.99.5 wti
.TP
Enroll a Linux host with pam_radius_auth, then switch it to pam_tacplus:
.nf
.B "  tacctl host enroll admin@web1 --method radius"
.B "  tacctl host enroll admin@web1 --method tacplus"
.fi
.TP
Bind the listeners to a specific management IP:
.nf
.B "  tacctl config listen tcp 10.1.0.1:49"
.B "  tacctl config listen --backend radius --listener auth udp 10.1.0.1:1812"
.fi
.TP
Check, without writing anything, whether a tacquito.yaml can move into the store:
.B tacctl store import --check
.TP
Expose the Prometheus exporter to an external scraper (default is loopback-only):
.B tacctl config metrics address 10.1.0.1:8080
.TP
Enable passwordless invocation for members of the adm group:
.B tacctl config sudoers install adm
.TP
Print client-side bcrypt generation instructions to share with a user:
.B tacctl hash commands
.SH NOTES
.B Unified tacctl config.
Every tacctl tunable \(em bcrypt cost, password / secret minimum lengths,
password-age warning threshold, default scope, mgmt-acl permits + ACL names,
and per-group Cisco priv-exec mappings \(em is driven by two sources:
the canonical defaults built into tacctl
(printed by
.BR "tacctl config defaults" )
and operator overrides in
.IR /etc/tacctl/tacctl.yaml .
Missing keys inherit from the defaults; setting a key back to the default
value removes it; an empty overrides file is pruned. Writes are
schema-validated (bad ranges, typo'd keys, malformed CIDRs rejected
before anything lands on disk).
.PP
.B IPv4 only by default.
The TACACS+ listener runs with
.B -network tcp
and the RADIUS ones with
.BR udp ,
which bind IPv4 only. Switching to
.B tcp6
or
.B udp6
enables a dual-stack socket on most platforms; IPv4 clients then
connect with IPv4-mapped addresses
.RB ( ::ffff:10.1.0.1 )
that do not match IPv4 prefix ACLs configured via
.BR "tacctl scope prefixes" ,
.BR "config allow" ,
or
.BR "config deny" .
Add equivalent IPv6 prefix rules before enabling
.B tcp6.
.PP
.B Juniper template users are required.
Every
.B local-user-name
referenced by a group class (for example
.BR RO-CLASS ,
.BR OP-CLASS ,
.BR RW-CLASS )
must have a matching local user on each Juniper device. Without this,
authentication succeeds but Junos rejects the login silently.
Run
.B tacctl config juniper
to regenerate the device-side configuration after adding or editing
groups.
.PP
.B Config edits.
.B tacctl
creates a snapshot before every change and restarts what the change
affects. Generated files
.RI ( tacquito.yaml ,
the unit drop-ins, the RADIUS files) are overwritten; a hand edit of one is
drift and blocks every change until it is adopted or discarded (see
.BR "STORE AND GENERATED CONFIGURATION" ).
.PP
.B Built on the host.
tacctl is a single Go binary. It is built on the server from the sources in
the clone
.I /opt/tacctl
(its dependencies are vendored there: no network or module proxy is used for
the build) with the Go toolchain in
.IR /usr/local/go ,
which
.I /opt/tacctl/bin/tacctl.sh
installs when it is missing, after checking the published SHA-256 of the
download. The first build compiles the standard library and the dependencies
(about 15 seconds on an eight-core machine, about 130 MB of Go build cache);
later builds reuse the build cache of root
.RI ( /root/.cache/go-build ).
.B "tacctl version \-\-long"
prints the commit and build date of the installed binary, which equals the
commit of the clone after every
.BR "tacctl install" " or " "tacctl upgrade" .
